export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"Which one of the following Carnatic music ragas is similar to Raga Bilawal in Hindustani music?","A":"Nat Bhairavi","B":"Kamavardhini","C":"Hanumatodi","D":"Dheera Shankarabharanam","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Art & Culture","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Dheera Shankarabharanam is the Carnatic equivalent of Bilawal in Hindustani classical music.\nBoth are based on the major scale structure with all natural notes.\nIn Carnatic music, Shankarabharanam is the 29th Melakarta raga.\nRaga Bilawal corresponds closely to the Bilawal thaat of Hindustani music.","revisionPoints":"Dheera Shankarabharanam → Bilawal\nShankarabharanam → 29th Melakarta\nBilawal → Hindustani classical music\nBoth use the natural-note/major-scale framework.\nUPSC can ask about equivalent ragas between Hindustani and Carnatic traditions."},
{"no":2,"text":"The artificially fixed rupee-sterling exchange rate prescribed by the Hilton-Young Commission (1926) was adopted by the British Government for which one of the following reasons?","A":"Aiding the flow of remittances from India and maintaining India's creditworthiness","B":"Providing support to Indian importers","C":"Encouraging export of cotton produce from India","D":"Preventing depreciation of the Rupee in terms of gold","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"History","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The Hilton-Young Commission was appointed in 1926 to examine India's currency and monetary system.\nIt recommended a fixed relationship between the rupee and sterling.\nThe exchange rate arrangement was favourable to British financial interests because it facilitated the transfer of funds and helped maintain India's ability to meet sterling obligations.\nIt therefore helped the British maintain the flow of remittances from India and India's creditworthiness.\nHence, (a) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Hilton-Young Commission → 1926\nConcerned with → Indian currency and monetary system\nRupee was linked to sterling.\nColonial monetary arrangements were closely connected with:\nHome Charges\nRemittances\nSterling obligations\nBritish financial interests\nQuick recall: Fixed rupee-sterling rate → British financial convenience."},
{"no":3,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Pali texts contain the first definite references to coins, e.g., kahapana, nikkha, kamsa, kakani.\nII. The literary evidence from Pali texts is corroborated by archaeological evidence of punch-marked coins from many sites, most of them made of silver.\nThe above statements have been associated with which of the following?\n1. Emergence of urban life\n2. Transition to money economy\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"History","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Pali texts contain references to various forms of coins and monetary transactions, indicating the growing importance of money in economic life.\nArchaeological discoveries of punch-marked coins, particularly silver coins, corroborate the literary evidence.\nThe increased use of coins is associated with the development of urban centres and the transition from a largely barter-based economy towards a money economy.\nTherefore, both emergence of urban life and transition to money economy are associated with the statements.","revisionPoints":"Early historic India → increasing use of coins.\nPali literature → references to:\nKahapana\nNikkha\nKamsa\nKakani\nPunch-marked coins → important archaeological evidence.\nCoinage is associated with:\nUrbanisation\nTrade\nCommercial expansion\nMoney economy\nQuick recall: Coins + towns + trade → second urbanisation."},
{"no":4,"text":"Which of the following temples has/have a Nagara-style shikhara?\n1. Malegitti Shivalaya, Badami\n2. Huchimalligudi Temple, Aihole\n3. Dashavatara Temple, Deogarh\n4. Virupaksha Temple, Pattadakal\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"3 only","D":"3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Art & Culture","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Huchimalligudi Temple at Aihole displays features associated with the Nagara tradition.\nDashavatara Temple at Deogarh is an important early example of the Nagara style, particularly its curvilinear shikhara tradition.\nThe Virupaksha Temple at Pattadakal is predominantly associated with the Dravida style.\nThe Malegitti Shivalaya is also not included among the temples identified here as having the relevant Nagara-style shikhara.\nTherefore, 2 and 3 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Nagara style → North Indian temple architecture.\nCharacteristic feature → curvilinear shikhara.\nDravida style → South Indian architecture → pyramidal vimana.\nDeogarh Dashavatara Temple → important early Nagara example.\nPattadakal → important centre showing both Nagara and Dravida architectural influences."},
{"no":5,"text":"Among the four main forms of existence of life recognized in Jainism, which one of the following is not included?","A":"Deva (gods)","B":"Yaksha (demi-gods)","C":"Manushya (humans)","D":"Tiryanch (animals and plants)","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Art & Culture","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Jainism broadly recognises four gatis (destinies/forms of existence):\nDeva → celestial beings/gods\nManushya → human beings\nTiryanch → animals, plants and other non-human living beings\nNaraka → hell beings\nYaksha is not one of these four principal gatis.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (b) Yaksha.","revisionPoints":"The four Jain gatis are:\nDeva → celestial beings\nManushya → humans\nTiryanch → animals/plants\nNaraka → hell beings\nYaksha → not one of the four gatis.\nJainism recognises the existence of jiva (living souls) in numerous forms."},
{"no":6,"text":"The Hallisalasya painting in the Bagh Caves represents:","A":"A joyous folk dance","B":"Buddha in a meditative pose","C":"The depiction of Shiva and Parvati on Kailasha","D":"Samudramanthan (Churning of the Ocean)","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"History","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The Bagh Caves are located in present-day Madhya Pradesh and are famous for their Buddhist paintings.\nThe Hallisalasya painting depicts a joyous dance, representing a lively social/cultural scene.\nBagh paintings are known for their expressive human figures, movement and use of rich colours.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (a).","revisionPoints":"Bagh Caves → Madhya Pradesh\nAssociated with → Buddhist art\nFamous for → mural paintings\nHallisalasya → joyous folk dance\nBagh paintings show strong similarities with the artistic tradition seen at Ajanta."},
{"no":7,"text":"Consider the following statements relating to the use of the place-value system in India:\n1. The earliest epigraphic use of the place-value system in India is found in the Mankani plates from Gujarat (AD 595–596).\n2. In the ninth century, place-values became general in inscriptions all over India.\n3. The place-values have been found in Sanskrit inscriptions in South-east Asia as early as the seventh century.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"History","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The place-value system was an important development in the history of Indian mathematics.\nThe Mankani plates of Gujarat, dated to AD 595–596, provide early epigraphic evidence of the system.\nBy the ninth century, place-value notation had become increasingly widespread in Indian inscriptions.\nThe system also spread beyond India, with evidence from Sanskrit inscriptions in Southeast Asia dating to the seventh century.\nHence, all three statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"India made major contributions to:\nDecimal system\nPlace-value notation\nConcept of zero\nMathematical computation\nMankani plates → Gujarat → AD 595–596\nIndian numerical knowledge spread to Southeast Asia.\nQuick recall: Indian mathematics → place value + zero + decimal tradition."},
{"no":8,"text":"Consider the following statements about the archaeological findings in Harappan towns:\nI. There is wide occurrence of spindle-whorls in the houses but absence of spinning wheels.\nII. Weights and measurement scales, complete with graduations have been discovered.\nIII. There are houses built in large part with baked bricks, around relatively spacious courtyards, with their own wells, bathing platforms, and large rooms.\nWhich of the following inferences can be drawn from the above statements?\n1. Statement I suggests that spinning was a laborious activity done at home.\n2. Statement II suggests the extent of the scientific knowledge that the Harappans possessed.\n3. Statement III suggests the emergence of a common property system.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"History","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Inference 1 is correct: The widespread presence of spindle-whorls indicates spinning activity, and the absence of spinning wheels suggests that spinning was carried out using simpler hand-operated techniques, making it a labour-intensive household activity.\nInference 2 is correct: Standardised weights and measuring instruments demonstrate the Harappans' considerable knowledge of measurement and standardisation, important for trade and economic activity.\nInference 3 is incorrect: Large houses with private wells, courtyards and bathing facilities indicate differences in household organisation and possibly social differentiation; they do not establish the emergence of a common property system.\nTherefore, 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Harappan civilisation → evidence of:\nSpindle-whorls → textile production\nStandardised weights → trade and measurement\nBaked-brick houses → urban planning\nWells and bathing platforms → water management\nCourtyards → organised domestic architecture\nImportant: Archaeological evidence must not be stretched beyond what it actually establishes."},
{"no":9,"text":"Which one of the following statements about the Eka Movement and Bardoli Satyagraha is correct?","A":"The Eka Movement was throughout supported and organized by the Congress while Bardoli Satyagraha was initially independent of Congress influence and was only in the last stages supported by the Congress.","B":"The Eka Movement was provided leadership by the taluqdars of Awadh, whereas the Bardoli Satyagraha was a movement of the landless labourers.","C":"The Bardoli Satyagraha was a campaign against the enhancement of land revenue, while the Eka Movement was a protest against excessive extraction of rents.","D":"The Eka Movement was located in the Varanasi and Mirzapur districts of the present-day U.P., while the Bardoli Satyagraha took place in Saurashtra.","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"History","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The Bardoli Satyagraha of 1928 in Gujarat was led by Vallabhbhai Patel against the enhancement of land revenue imposed by the colonial government.\nThe Eka Movement (1921–22) emerged in parts of present-day Uttar Pradesh and involved peasants protesting against high rents and oppressive revenue-related demands.\nTherefore, option (c) correctly distinguishes the central issue of the two movements.\nThe other options contain incorrect descriptions of their leadership, geography or social composition.","revisionPoints":"Bardoli Satyagraha → 1928 → Gujarat\nLeader → Vallabhbhai Patel\nMain issue → land-revenue enhancement\nEka Movement → 1921–22 → Uttar Pradesh\nMain concern → excessive rents and peasant exploitation\nBardoli → Vallabhbhai Patel earned the title \"Sardar\"."},
{"no":10,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Rigvedic period:\nI. Irrigation from wells allowed agriculture to expand away from flood plains and strips on river margins into the present Punjab and Haryana plains having underground water levels reasonably close to the surface.\nII. Draught-animal power was employed to draw up water out of the wells.\nWhich of the following information support/supports the above statements?\n1. There is evidence in the Rigveda of the use of ashma chakra (stone pulley wheel) and daha (strapped wooden pails) to draw up water.\n2. Mention has been made in the Rigveda of the use of implements like parashu/kulisha (axe) and datra/sreni (sickle).\n3. There is a history of the use of ox, even before the Rigveda, for ploughing the land and pulling the carts.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 supports the above statements: References to devices such as the ashma chakra and water-drawing containers provide evidence of techniques used for drawing water from wells.\nStatement 3 supports the statements: The use of oxen for agricultural work and pulling carts establishes the availability of draught-animal power, which could facilitate agricultural and water-management activities.\nStatement 2 does not directly support the statements: The parashu/kulisha and datra/sreni are agricultural implements, but their existence does not specifically establish the use of wells or draught animals for lifting water.\nTherefore, 1 and 3 only support the given statements.","revisionPoints":"Rigvedic economy was predominantly pastoral-agricultural.\nImportant agricultural implements:\nParashu/Kulisha → axe\nDatra/Sreni → sickle\nEvidence of:\nWells\nWater-drawing devices\nOxen\nPloughing\nQuick recall: When UPSC asks which evidence supports a statement, distinguish direct evidence from merely related information."},
{"no":11,"text":"Consider the following assertion:\nIn the Pleistocene period either the Yamuna once flowed into the Indus, or the Sutlej flowed into the Yamuna and one major tributary of either had shifted from the Ganga to the Indus or vice versa.\nWhich of the following is/are the basis of the above assertion?\n1. The Nadi-Sukta of the Rigveda\n2. The explorations of the Sutlej and the Yamuna by Robert Bruce Foote\n3. The presence of the same species of dolphins in both the Indus and the Ganga river systems\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2","D":"3","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geography","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The assertion about the possible shifting of ancient river courses between the Indus and Ganga systems is supported by the presence of the same species of dolphins in both river systems.\nThe Indus river dolphin and the Ganga river dolphin are closely related freshwater dolphins. Their distribution provides evidence that there may have been historical connections between the river systems, allowing aquatic species to move between them.\nThe Nadi-Sukta of the Rigveda provides information about rivers known to the Vedic people, but it does not constitute the specific basis for the Pleistocene river-course assertion mentioned in the question.\nThe explorations of Robert Bruce Foote are also not the basis identified for this particular assertion.\nTherefore, Statement 3 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"Pleistocene period → major climatic and geographical changes.\nAncient river courses can be studied through geological, geomorphological, biological and archaeological evidence.\nThe distribution of freshwater species can provide clues about past connections between river systems.\nIndus and Ganga river systems → evidence from freshwater dolphins.\nNadi-Sukta → Rigveda → important source for ancient river geography.\nRobert Bruce Foote was an important pioneer of prehistoric archaeology and geology in India.\nUPSC may connect river systems, palaeogeography, fossils, species distribution and geological evidence."},
{"no":12,"text":"What does an empty seat represent in early Buddhist iconography?\nOptions:","A":"The meditation of the Buddha","B":"The Buddha's First Sermon","C":"The Buddha's Mahaparinibbana","D":"The Buddha's Mahabhinishkramana","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Art & Culture","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Early Buddhist art often represented the Buddha through symbols rather than a human figure. This is generally referred to as the aniconic phase of Buddhist art.\nThe empty seat or throne represents the presence of the Buddha in meditation without depicting him in human form.\nOther important symbols were also used to represent events or aspects of the Buddha's life. The Dharma Chakra is associated with his teaching and First Sermon, while the Bodhi tree represents his enlightenment.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (a) The meditation of the Buddha.","revisionPoints":"Empty seat/throne → Buddha's meditation/presence.\nBodhi tree → Enlightenment.\nDharma Chakra → First Sermon / turning of the Wheel of Dharma.\nBuddha's footprints → symbolic presence of the Buddha.\nStupa → Buddhist sacred and commemorative monument.\nEarly Buddhist art is characterised by aniconic representation.\nLater Buddhist art increasingly represented the Buddha in human form.\nImportant Buddhist art traditions: Gandhara, Mathura and Amaravati."},
{"no":13,"text":"Which of the following pairs of ancient and modern names of rivers is/are correctly matched?\n1. Vitasta — Chenab\n2. Asikni — Jhelum\n3. Parushni — Ravi\n4. Yayavati — Beas\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 and 2","B":"3 and 4","C":"3 only","D":"4 only","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geography","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The ancient names of several rivers of north-western India are mentioned in Vedic literature.\nStatement 1 is incorrect: Vitasta refers to the Jhelum, not the Chenab.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: Asikni refers to the Chenab, not the Jhelum.\nStatement 3 is correct: Parushni refers to the Ravi.\nStatement 4 is incorrect: The Beas is generally identified with Vipas/Vipasha, not Yayavati.\nTherefore, only Statement 3 is correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Vitasta → Jhelum.\nAsikni → Chenab.\nParushni → Ravi.\nVipas/Vipasha → Beas.\nShutudri → Sutlej.\nSindhu → Indus.\nSarasvati → Sarasvati/Ghaggar-Hakra system in historical-geographical discussions.\nAncient river names are important for understanding Vedic geography.\nUPSC frequently uses ancient name ↔ modern name matching questions."},
{"no":14,"text":"Which of the following statements on the Amaravati Stupa and its relief sculpture is/are correct?\n1. It was located in the lower Krishna valley.\n2. In India, it was next only to the Sanchi Stupa in size.\n3. The Amaravati school of sculpture made a lasting impact on the later South Indian sculpture, and its products were carried to Sri Lanka and South-East Asia.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Art & Culture","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The Amaravati Stupa was located in the lower Krishna valley in present-day Andhra Pradesh.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: The statement that the Amaravati Stupa was second only to the Sanchi Stupa in size is not correct.\nStatement 3 is correct: The Amaravati school of sculpture had a lasting influence on later South Indian sculpture. Its artistic influence also spread beyond India, particularly to Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 3 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Amaravati → Lower Krishna Valley → Andhra Pradesh.\nAmaravati was an important centre of Buddhist architecture and sculpture.\nAmaravati is famous for its relief sculptures.\nSculptures depict Buddhist themes, Jataka stories and episodes associated with the Buddha.\nAmaravati sculpture is known for narrative reliefs, movement and detailed representation.\nAmaravati artistic influence spread to Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia.\nImportant Buddhist art centres: Sanchi, Bharhut, Amaravati, Gandhara and Mathura.\nUPSC may compare Buddhist sites on the basis of location, architecture, sculpture and artistic features."},
{"no":15,"text":"Which of the following pairs of the king and his dynasty in early historical Tamilakam is/are not correctly matched?\n1. Senguttuvan — Chera\n2. Udayanjeral — Chola\n3. Nedunjeliyan — Pandya\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"History","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Senguttuvan was a famous ruler of the Chera dynasty and is prominently associated with the Tamil epic Silappadikaram.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: Udayanjeral belonged to the Chera dynasty, not the Chola dynasty.\nStatement 3 is correct: Nedunjeliyan was associated with the Pandya dynasty.\nTherefore, only Statement 2 is incorrectly matched.","revisionPoints":"Senguttuvan → Chera.\nUdayanjeral → Chera.\nNedunjeliyan → Pandya.\nThe three major ruling houses of early Tamilakam were Chera, Chola and Pandya.\nChera → western Tamilakam and Kerala region.\nChola → Kaveri region.\nPandya → southern Tamilakam, especially Madurai region.\nImportant Sangam literature includes Ettuthokai and Pattuppattu.\nSilappadikaram and Manimekalai are important Tamil epics.\nUPSC may ask ruler → dynasty → region → literary source."},
{"no":16,"text":"Which of the following factors contributed to the formation of the Forward Bloc by Subhas Chandra Bose in 1939?\n1. Bose failed to win the confidence of Mahatma Gandhi.\n2. The Congress Left was disunited and failed to support Bose.\n3. The Communists did not support Bose in his endeavours.\n4. The supporters of M. N. Roy and socialist leaders like Jayaprakash Narayan preferred Congress unity to supporting Bose.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1, 2 and 4","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"2 and 4 only","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"History","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The Forward Bloc was founded by Subhas Chandra Bose in 1939 following serious differences within the Indian National Congress.\nBose had been re-elected Congress President in the Tripuri Session of 1939, but he faced strong opposition from the group associated with Mahatma Gandhi and the Congress leadership.\nStatement 1 is correct: Bose failed to obtain the confidence and cooperation of Gandhi and the dominant Congress leadership.\nStatement 2 is correct: The Congress Left was divided and did not provide united support to Bose.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: The statement that the Communists simply did not support Bose is not accepted as a factor in the manner stated.\nStatement 4 is correct: Supporters associated with M.N. Roy and socialist leaders such as Jayaprakash Narayan placed importance on maintaining broader Congress unity rather than supporting Bose's position.\nTherefore, 1, 2 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Forward Bloc → Subhas Chandra Bose → 1939.\nFormation followed internal differences within the Indian National Congress.\nTripuri Congress Session → 1939.\nBose was re-elected Congress President in 1939.\nHis re-election created serious differences with the Gandhian group.\nBose subsequently resigned as Congress President.\nThe Forward Bloc aimed to consolidate left-wing and radical nationalist forces.\nImportant associated personalities: Subhas Chandra Bose, M.N. Roy and Jayaprakash Narayan.\nRevise the Tripuri Crisis, Congress Left, Forward Bloc and Bose's political strategy."},
{"no":17,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the British policy in Awadh immediately after its annexation in 1856:\n1. The taluqdars were dispossessed of their estates but allowed to retain their arms and forts.\n2. A Summary Revenue Settlement was made in 1856 assuming that the taluqdars were outsiders.\n3. The British believed in taking revenue directly from the peasants by removing the taluqdars.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?\nOptions:","A":"2 and 3 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"2 only","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"History","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Awadh was annexed by the British in 1856 under Lord Dalhousie. After annexation, the British introduced changes in the revenue and administrative system that weakened the traditional position of the taluqdars.\nStatement 1 is incorrect: Although the taluqdars were dispossessed of their estates, the statement that they were allowed to retain their arms and forts is incorrect.\nStatement 2 is correct: The British introduced a Summary Settlement of Revenue in 1856, treating the taluqdars as intermediaries or outsiders in the new revenue arrangement.\nStatement 3 is correct: British officials attempted to establish a more direct relationship with the cultivators for revenue collection by reducing the authority of the taluqdars.\nThese policies created considerable resentment among the taluqdars and contributed to the support for the Revolt of 1857 in Awadh.","revisionPoints":"Awadh annexed → 1856.\nAnnexation took place under Lord Dalhousie.\nTaluqdars were major landed intermediaries in Awadh.\nBritish revenue policy weakened the traditional position of taluqdars.\nSummary Settlement → 1856.\nBritish attempted to establish revenue relations more directly with cultivators.\nDispossession of taluqdars generated widespread resentment.\nAwadh became an important centre of the Revolt of 1857.\nTaluqdars later played an important role in mobilising support during the revolt.\nImportant chain: Annexation of Awadh → Revenue changes → Dispossession of Taluqdars → Resentment → Revolt of 1857."},
{"no":18,"text":"Consider the following assertion:\nThe genesis of political alliances based on community lay in the very nature of the Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms, 1919.\nWhich of the following statements support/supports the above assertion?\n1. Reforms retained and extended the principle of separate electorates.\n2. Separate electorates were supposed to counter Indian nationalism, which was growing stronger.\n3. Deprived classes rallied around the favours inherent in separate electorates.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"History","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms of 1919, implemented through the Government of India Act, 1919, expanded constitutional and electoral arrangements in British India.\nStatement 1 is correct: The reforms retained and extended separate electorates, continuing the system of political representation based on community.\nStatement 2 is correct: Separate electorates formed part of the colonial strategy of dealing with India's growing nationalism by encouraging political representation along communal lines.\nStatement 3 is correct: Different disadvantaged or minority communities could seek political safeguards and representation through the benefits associated with separate electorates. This contributed to the emergence of political alliances based on community.\nTherefore, all three statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms → 1919.\nImplemented through the Government of India Act, 1919.\nIntroduced dyarchy in the provinces.\nExpanded legislative councils and electoral representation.\nSeparate electorates were retained and extended.\nSeparate electorates for Muslims had been introduced under the Morley-Minto Reforms, 1909.\nCommunal representation became an important feature of colonial constitutional politics.\nImportant sequence: Morley-Minto Reforms, 1909 → Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms, 1919 → Government of India Act, 1935.\nUPSC may connect constitutional reforms with communal representation, electoral politics and the growth of nationalism."},
{"no":19,"text":"Pandit Mallikarjun Mansur, the famous classical singer from Karnataka, represented the:\nOptions:","A":"Agra Gharana","B":"Gwalior Gharana","C":"Patiala Gharana","D":"Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Art & Culture","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Pandit Mallikarjun Mansur was a renowned Hindustani classical vocalist from Karnataka and was associated with the Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana.\nThe Jaipur-Atrauli tradition is particularly known for its treatment of complex and rare ragas and distinctive approaches to melodic structure.\nThe gharana was founded by Ustad Alladiya Khan.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (d) Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana.","revisionPoints":"Mallikarjun Mansur → Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana.\nMallikarjun Mansur was a renowned Hindustani classical vocalist.\nJaipur-Atrauli Gharana → Alladiya Khan.\nJaipur-Atrauli is known for complex and uncommon ragas.\nGwalior Gharana is one of the major and oldest khayal traditions.\nAgra Gharana has strong connections with khayal and dhrupad traditions.\nPatiala Gharana is known for its distinctive vocal style and rich repertoire.\nUPSC may ask about musician → gharana → state → musical tradition."},
{"no":20,"text":"In which one among the following texts does the term kshetra-patni (mistress of the field) originate?\nOptions:","A":"Rigveda","B":"Atharvaveda","C":"Ashtadhyayi","D":"Arthashastra","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"History","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The term kshetra-patni, meaning \"mistress of the field\", originates in the Atharvaveda.\nThe term is associated with agriculture, cultivated land and fertility. It reflects the importance of agricultural activity and the fertility of the land in Vedic society.\nThe Atharvaveda contains material dealing with several aspects of everyday life, including agriculture, health, fertility, prosperity and social concerns.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (b) Atharvaveda.","revisionPoints":"Kshetra-patni → Atharvaveda.\nKshetra means field or cultivated land.\nKshetra-patni means mistress of the field.\nThe term is associated with agriculture, fertility and cultivated land.\nRigveda → hymns and early Vedic religious traditions.\nSamaveda → chants and melodies.\nYajurveda → sacrificial formulae and rituals.\nAtharvaveda → everyday life, healing, charms, fertility and social concerns.\nAshtadhyayi → Panini → Sanskrit grammar.\nArthashastra → Kautilya → statecraft, administration and economy.\nUPSC may test specific terminology → original text/source → meaning."},
{"no":21,"text":"Consider the following statements with reference to India's response to climate change:\nI. India's Long-Term Low Emission Development Strategy (LT-LEDS) is a crucial tool for achieving net-zero emissions by 2070.\nII. India's 4th Biennial Update Report (BUR-4) submitted in December, 2024 recorded around 8% decrease in Greenhouse gas emissions in 2020 over 2019.\nIII. Climate-resilient development necessarily depends on quick and short-term achievement of emission reduction targets.\nWhich of the following relationships among the above statements is/are correct?\n1. Statement I is empirically supported by Statement II.\n2. Statement III contradicts the approach implicit in Statement I.\n3. Statement I and Statement III together establish the premise of long-term sustainability.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environment & Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: India's LT-LEDS is important for achieving Net Zero by 2070.  India has a target of Net Zero by 2070. LT-LEDS means Long-Term Low-Emission Development Strategy.It gives India a long-term plan to reduce emissions while continuing economic development.\nStatement 2 is correct: BUR-4 recorded around 8% decrease in greenhouse gas emissions in 2020 over 2019. BUR = Biennial Update Report. India submitted its 4th BUR (BUR-4) in December 2024. It reported a decrease in emissions in 2020 compared with 2019. One important reason was the COVID-19 pandemic, which reduced economic and transport activities.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Climate change cannot be addressed only through quick, short-term measures.\nTherefore, 2 and 3 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Net Zero Target of India → 2070\nNet Zero target announced → COP26, Glasgow (2021)\nLT-LEDS → Long-Term Low-Emission Development Strategy\nLT-LEDS submitted → COP27 (2022)\nPurpose of LT-LEDS → Long-term pathway for low-emission development\nBUR → Biennial Update Report\nBUR-4 → India's Fourth Biennial Update Report\nBUR-4 submitted → December 2024\n2020 GHG emissions → Declined compared with 2019\nMajor reason for 2020 decline → COVID-19-related slowdown\nClimate-resilient development → Mitigation + Adaptation + Sustainable Development\nClimate action → Requires long-term and sustained efforts\nImportant trap → Climate resilience does NOT depend only on quick, short-term emission reductions"},
{"no":22,"text":"With respect to the Western Hoolock Gibbons, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. A Sanctuary in North-east India is home to this ape species listed as Endangered in the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List.\n2. They have specialized brachiation and can easily swing between trees.\n3. They possess a strong and heavy build like gorillas, yet are remarkably agile tree climbers.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environment & Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The Western Hoolock Gibbon is found in the forests of northeastern India and is listed as Endangered on the IUCN Red List. Protected areas and sanctuaries in the region provide important habitats for this species.\nStatement 2 is correct: Hoolock gibbons are highly adapted to life in trees. They use brachiation, moving through the forest canopy by swinging from branch to branch using their long arms.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Hoolock gibbons do not have a strong and heavy build like gorillas. They are relatively slender and lightweight, which helps them move rapidly through the forest canopy.\nTherefore, 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Western Hoolock Gibbon → Northeast India.\nHoolock gibbons are apes, not monkeys.\nThey are highly adapted to arboreal life.\nBrachiation → swinging from branch to branch using the arms.\nHoolock gibbons are known for their agility in the forest canopy.\nWestern Hoolock Gibbon is listed as Endangered by the IUCN.\nImportant threats include habitat loss, fragmentation and hunting.\nNortheast India is an important region for primate diversity.\nUPSC may ask about endangered species through habitat, geographical distribution, IUCN status and adaptations."},
{"no":23,"text":"Which of the following best explain(s) the rationale for protecting mangrove ecosystems in the context of climate resilience?\n1. Mangroves reduce tidal energy and store freshwater, making them ideal sites for paddy cultivation in saline estuarine belts.\n2. Their salt-sensitive roots filter seawater, making mangroves key to converting coastal land into freshwater aquaculture zones.\n3. By withstanding tidal surges and offering biomass resources, mangroves function both as natural bio-shields and livelihood bases for rural communities.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environment & Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Mangroves can reduce wave and tidal energy and help protect coastlines, but they do not generally store freshwater in a way that makes saline estuarine areas ideal for paddy cultivation.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: Mangroves are salt-tolerant, not salt-sensitive. Their specialised root systems help them survive saline and waterlogged environments, but they do not convert coastal land into freshwater aquaculture zones.\nStatement 3 is correct: Mangroves act as natural bio-shields by reducing the impact of cyclones, storm surges, waves and coastal erosion. They also provide resources and livelihoods through fisheries, honey, fuelwood and other ecosystem services.\nTherefore, Statement 3 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"Mangroves → tropical and subtropical intertidal coastal ecosystems.\nMangroves are adapted to saline and waterlogged conditions.\nThey help reduce coastal erosion and wave energy.\nMangroves provide protection against storm surges and cyclones.\nThey are important carbon sinks and store significant amounts of carbon.\nMangroves support fish, crustaceans, birds and other biodiversity.\nThey provide livelihood support to coastal communities.\nImportant Indian mangrove regions include Sundarbans, Mahanadi delta, Godavari-Krishna delta and Gulf of Kachchh.\nUPSC may connect mangroves with blue carbon, coastal resilience, biodiversity and climate adaptation."},
{"no":24,"text":"In what way(s) does the Vizhinjam International Seaport represent a structural shift in India's maritime trade and logistics policy?\n1. By functioning exclusively as a domestic cargo hub to reduce reliance on coastal shipping and eliminate the need for foreign collaborations.\n2. By focusing primarily on passenger cruise tourism and heritage shipping to increase Kerala's profile as a maritime heritage destination.\n3. By leveraging its natural deep draft and strategic location to reduce dependence on foreign trans-shipment ports, enhance revenue retention, and reposition India in regional maritime trade.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Vizhinjam is not exclusively a domestic cargo hub. Its importance lies particularly in its potential as an international trans-shipment hub.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: Its primary strategic importance is not passenger cruise tourism or maritime heritage. The port is significant because of its location, deep natural draft and proximity to major international shipping routes.\nStatement 3 is correct: Vizhinjam's natural deep-water characteristics and location close to major international shipping routes can enable India to handle more trans-shipment cargo domestically rather than relying heavily on foreign ports. This can improve logistics efficiency and help retain more maritime-related revenue within India.\nTherefore, Statement 3 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"Vizhinjam International Seaport → Kerala.\nLocated near Thiruvananthapuram.\nMajor strategic advantage: natural deep draft.\nLocated close to major international east-west maritime shipping routes.\nImportant objective: strengthen India's trans-shipment capacity.\nTrans-shipment means transferring cargo from one vessel to another at an intermediate port.\nGreater domestic trans-shipment capacity can reduce dependence on foreign trans-shipment hubs.\nImportant themes: Sagarmala, port-led development, maritime logistics and trans-shipment.\nUPSC may connect ports with location, natural advantages, shipping routes and strategic importance."},
{"no":25,"text":"Identify the river of the Indian sub-continent on the basis of the following information:\n1. It has an antecedent drainage system.\n2. It flows through three countries.\n3. It originates in the Tibetan Plateau and is an important river for irrigation.\n4. It does not form distributaries.\nSelect the answer from the following:\nOptions:","A":"Brahmaputra","B":"Indus","C":"Sutlej","D":"Teesta","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geography","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The Sutlej is a major river of the Indus river system. It originates in the Tibetan Plateau and flows through China, India and Pakistan.\nIt is an antecedent river, meaning that its course existed before the uplift of the surrounding mountain ranges and was able to maintain its course by cutting through the rising terrain.\nThe Sutlej is an important source of water for irrigation and hydropower. Unlike rivers such as the Ganga and Brahmaputra, the Sutlej does not form a large delta with numerous distributaries.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (c) Sutlej.","revisionPoints":"Sutlej → Indus river system.\nAncient name: Shutudri/Sutudri.\nOrigin → Tibetan Plateau.\nFlows through China, India and Pakistan.\nAntecedent drainage → river maintains its course despite uplift of surrounding terrain.\nSutlej is an important river for irrigation and hydropower.\nIt is one of the important rivers associated with the Indus river system.\nIndus → Pakistan and India, with its upper course in Tibet/China.\nBrahmaputra → Tibet, India and Bangladesh.\nUPSC may test rivers through origin, countries, tributaries, drainage type and delta/distributary formation."},
{"no":26,"text":"Which of the following with reference to Indian States is/are not correct?\n1. Uttar Pradesh shares its boundary with the highest number of other Indian States.\n2. Rajasthan shares the longest international border among all Indian States.\n3. Sikkim is the only State that shares its boundary with just one other Indian State.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geography","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Uttar Pradesh shares boundaries with eight Indian States and one Union Territory, making it the Indian state sharing boundaries with the highest number of other states.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: West Bengal, not Rajasthan, has the longest international boundary among Indian states.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Sikkim is not the only state sharing its boundary with just one other Indian state. Other states also have boundaries with only one neighbouring Indian state.\nTherefore, Statements 2 and 3 only are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Uttar Pradesh → borders 8 Indian States + Delhi.\nUP's neighbouring states include Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh, Haryana, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand and Bihar.\nWest Bengal → longest international boundary among Indian states.\nWest Bengal borders Bangladesh, Bhutan and Nepal internationally.\nSikkim → borders West Bengal and four countries/regions internationally: Nepal, Bhutan and China/Tibet.\nIndia's geographical boundaries are important for understanding international borders and interstate boundaries.\nUPSC may ask about states sharing the maximum number of states, longest borders and unique geographical boundaries."},
{"no":27,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to the arrival of Amur Falcons at Doyang Lake in Nagaland each year from Mongolia is/are correct?\n1. It showcases how sustained local conservation efforts can contribute to the arrival and protection of international migratory birds.\n2. It reflects the global success of advanced tracking technologies that guide migratory birds back to their stopover sites.\n3. It confirms that Amur Falcons have adapted to permanent residency in India due to favourable habitat changes.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environment & Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The annual arrival of Amur Falcons at Doyang Lake in Nagaland demonstrates the importance of local conservation efforts in protecting migratory birds. Community participation and conservation initiatives have helped protect these birds during their migration.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: The annual migration is primarily a result of the species' natural migratory behaviour and ecological cues, not evidence that advanced tracking technologies guide the birds back to their stopover sites.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Amur Falcons are migratory birds, not permanent residents of India. They visit India as part of their long-distance migration.\nTherefore, Statement 1 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"Amur Falcon → long-distance migratory bird.\nDoyang Lake → Nagaland.\nAmur Falcons migrate through India during their annual journey.\nThey undertake one of the world's notable long-distance bird migrations.\nLocal communities have played an important role in their conservation.\nCommunity-based conservation can protect migratory species and stopover habitats.\nMigratory birds depend on a network of breeding grounds, stopover sites and wintering grounds.\nImportant concept: Migration ≠ permanent residency.\nUPSC may connect migratory birds with flyways, wetlands, habitat protection and community conservation."},
{"no":28,"text":"Which among the following is/are the objective(s) of the Rainfed Area Development (RAD) initiative under the National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA)?\n1. Encouraging monoculture in rainfed areas\n2. Increasing rice cultivation in irrigated regions\n3. Enhancing productivity and minimising climatic risks through Integrated Farming Systems (IFS)\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The Rainfed Area Development (RAD) component under the National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA) aims to improve the productivity and resilience of rainfed agricultural systems.\nStatement 1 is incorrect: RAD does not promote monoculture. It encourages diversification and Integrated Farming Systems to improve resilience and sustainability.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: RAD is not aimed at increasing rice cultivation specifically in irrigated regions.\nStatement 3 is correct: RAD promotes Integrated Farming Systems (IFS) involving combinations such as crops, livestock, horticulture and other suitable activities. This helps increase productivity, diversify income and reduce climatic risks.\nTherefore, Statement 3 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"RAD → Rainfed Area Development.\nRAD is a component of the National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA).\nFocus → rainfed agricultural areas.\nPromotes Integrated Farming Systems (IFS).\nObjectives include productivity enhancement and climate-risk reduction.\nIntegrated farming can combine crops, livestock, horticulture, agroforestry and other activities.\nDiversification can improve farm resilience and income stability.\nImportant distinction: Monoculture → concentration on a single crop; Integrated Farming → diversified farming activities.\nUPSC may connect RAD with sustainable agriculture, rainfed farming, climate resilience and NMSA."},
{"no":29,"text":"Which of the following is/are the most significant implication(s) of obtaining Oeko-Tex certification for Eri Silk in the global textiles industry?\n1. It allows Indian exporters to compete in high-end markets that prioritise chemical-free products.\n2. It confirms that Eri Silk meets international safety, environmental and quality standards, enabling its entry into premium eco-conscious markets.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Oeko-Tex certification is associated with standards relating to textile safety and harmful substances. Obtaining such certification can improve the marketability of Indian textiles in international markets where consumers and buyers increasingly demand safer and environmentally responsible products.\nStatement 1 is correct: Certification can improve the ability of Indian exporters to access and compete in premium textile markets where chemical safety and sustainability are important considerations.\nStatement 2 is correct: Oeko-Tex certification indicates compliance with specified textile safety requirements and standards, helping build consumer and buyer confidence in the product.\nTherefore, both Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Eri silk → important silk variety of India.\nEri silk is particularly associated with Northeastern India, especially Assam and neighbouring regions.\nEri silk is also known as Endi/Errandi silk in some contexts.\nEri silkworm is associated with Samia ricini.\nEri silk is traditionally associated with open-ended cocoon processing, allowing the moth to emerge.\nOEKO-TEX → textile safety and harmful-substance testing/certification framework.\nCertification can improve market access and consumer confidence.\nImportant themes: sustainable textiles, natural fibres, certification, exports and eco-conscious markets.\nUPSC may connect traditional Indian products with GI tags, certification, sustainability and international markets."},
{"no":30,"text":"Ships from which of the following countries have to cross the Strait of Hormuz to reach out to the Indian Ocean?\n1. Bahrain\n2. Syria\n3. Qatar\n4. Egypt\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 and 2","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geography","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The Strait of Hormuz connects the Persian Gulf with the Gulf of Oman and the Arabian Sea, providing access to the Indian Ocean.\nBahrain and Qatar are located in the Persian Gulf. Ships travelling from these countries to the open Indian Ocean generally have to pass through the Strait of Hormuz.\nSyria is located on the Mediterranean coast and does not require passage through the Strait of Hormuz to reach the Indian Ocean.\nEgypt has access to the Mediterranean Sea and the Red Sea through the Suez Canal, so ships from Egypt do not need to cross the Strait of Hormuz to reach the Indian Ocean.\nTherefore, 1 and 3 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Strait of Hormuz → Persian Gulf ↔ Gulf of Oman.\nIt provides access from the Persian Gulf to the Arabian Sea and Indian Ocean.\nImportant countries around the Persian Gulf include Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, Bahrain, Qatar and the UAE.\nBahrain and Qatar → Persian Gulf → Strait of Hormuz → Arabian Sea → Indian Ocean.\nSyria → Mediterranean Sea.\nEgypt → Mediterranean Sea + Red Sea, connected by the Suez Canal.\nStrait of Hormuz is strategically important because of the movement of large volumes of oil and energy shipments.\nImportant maritime chokepoints to revise: Strait of Hormuz, Strait of Malacca, Bab-el-Mandeb, Suez Canal and Panama Canal.\nUPSC may test chokepoints through location, connecting water bodies, neighbouring countries and strategic importance."},
{"no":31,"text":"Tungurahua Volcano, which was declared a Global Geopark by UNESCO in 2025, is situated in which one among the following countries?\nOptions:","A":"Ecuador","B":"Peru","C":"Bolivia","D":"Colombia","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geography","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Tungurahua Volcano is located in Ecuador, in the central part of the country. It is an active stratovolcano situated in the Andes.\nThe Tungurahua region is associated with volcanic landscapes, geothermal features and biodiversity. Ecuador is part of the Pacific Ring of Fire, making it a region of significant volcanic and seismic activity.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (a) Ecuador.","revisionPoints":"Tungurahua Volcano → Ecuador.\nEcuador is located in the Andes mountain system.\nEcuador lies along the Pacific Ring of Fire.\nStratovolcano → steep-sided volcanic cone generally built from successive layers of lava and volcanic material.\nImportant Ecuadorian volcanoes: Cotopaxi, Chimborazo and Tungurahua.\nUPSC may ask volcanic features through location, tectonic setting, type of volcano and associated geographical phenomena.\nRing of Fire → Pacific Ocean margins → intense seismic and volcanic activity."},
{"no":32,"text":"With reference to Madhav National Park, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. It was declared a Tiger Reserve in India in 2025.\n2. Sakhya Sagar, which is designated as a Ramsar Site, is situated within this National Park.\n3. Its area is shared between Madhya Pradesh and Rajasthan.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environment & Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Madhav National Park in Madhya Pradesh was declared a Tiger Reserve in 2025, adding it to India's network of tiger reserves.\nStatement 2 is correct: Sakhya Sagar Lake is located within Madhav National Park. It is an important wetland and has been designated as a Ramsar Site.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Madhav National Park is located in Madhya Pradesh and is not an area shared between Madhya Pradesh and Rajasthan.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Madhav National Park → Madhya Pradesh.\nLocated in the Shivpuri district.\nMadhav National Park → Tiger Reserve in 2025.\nSakhya Sagar → Ramsar Site → within Madhav National Park.\nThe park is part of the Madhya Pradesh protected-area network.\nImportant species associated with the landscape include tiger, leopard, chital, sambar and crocodiles.\nUPSC may connect National Park → State → Tiger Reserve status → Ramsar Site → important species."},
{"no":33,"text":"With reference to the climate of Andaman and Nicobar Islands, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. The climate can be defined as a humid, tropical coastal climate.\n2. It receives rainfall from both South-west monsoon and North-east monsoon.\n3. Maximum precipitation is between December and May.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geography","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The Andaman and Nicobar Islands have a humid tropical maritime/coastal climate, influenced strongly by their location in the Bay of Bengal.\nStatement 2 is correct: The islands receive rainfall from both the South-west Monsoon and the North-east Monsoon. Their maritime location also contributes to high humidity and rainfall.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: The maximum rainfall is not concentrated between December and May. The major rainfall season is associated primarily with the monsoon months, particularly the South-west Monsoon period.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Andaman and Nicobar Islands → tropical maritime climate.\nHigh temperature and humidity throughout much of the year.\nReceives rainfall from South-west Monsoon and North-east Monsoon.\nSurrounded by the Bay of Bengal/Andaman Sea region.\nThe islands experience significant rainfall because of their maritime and monsoonal setting.\nNorth-east Monsoon is particularly important for parts of southern India and the southeast coast, but the Andaman and Nicobar Islands can also receive rainfall from it.\nUPSC may ask about island climates through monsoon direction, rainfall seasonality and maritime influence."},
{"no":34,"text":"Which of the following geographical features or phenomena is/are associated with the Peninsular Block of India?\n1. Submergence of parts of the western coast due to tectonic activity.\n2. Presence of residual mountain ranges such as the Veliconda Hills and Mahendragiri Hills.\n3. Deep, V-shaped river valleys formed by fast-flowing rivers.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geography","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The Peninsular Block is one of the oldest and most stable geological regions of India. It is composed largely of ancient crystalline rocks and has undergone long periods of erosion and tectonic modification.\nStatement 1 is correct: Parts of the western coast have experienced submergence and tectonic changes, contributing to the characteristic coastal features of the western margin.\nStatement 2 is correct: Old and residual mountain ranges and uplands, including the Velikonda Hills and Mahendragiri Hills, are associated with the Peninsular region.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Deep, narrow V-shaped valleys are more characteristic of youthful, rapidly eroding mountainous regions. Much of the Peninsular drainage is associated with an old and mature landscape.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Peninsular Block → one of India's oldest geological regions.\nDominated by ancient crystalline rocks.\nIncludes parts of the Deccan Plateau and other plateau regions.\nImportant ranges/features: Western Ghats, Eastern Ghats, Nilgiris, Mahendragiri and Velikonda Hills.\nThe Peninsular region has experienced long-term erosion and denudation.\nRivers of the Peninsular Plateau are generally older than Himalayan rivers.\nImportant Peninsular rivers include Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri, Mahanadi, Narmada and Tapi.\nNarmada and Tapi → west-flowing Peninsular rivers.\nUPSC may compare Peninsular Block vs Himalayan region on the basis of age, geology, relief and drainage."},
{"no":35,"text":"Consider the following statements with reference to the Sagarmala Programme of the Government of India:\nI. The Sagarmala Programme seeks to achieve port-led economic growth through cost-effective and sustainable coastal infrastructure.\nII. The success of the Sagarmala Programme is reflected in significant growth in coastal and inland waterway shipping, along with improved global port rankings.\nIII. Sagarmala 2.0 aims to position India as a global maritime innovation hub aligned with Atmanirbhar Bharat and Viksit Bharat 2047 visions.\nWhich of the following relationships among the above statements is/are correct?\n1. Statement II validates the effectiveness of the strategies envisioned in Statement I.\n2. Statement III extends the objectives of Statement I by embedding them into a future-oriented innovation framework.\n3. Statement I contradicts Statement III by focusing only on traditional infrastructure instead of modern innovation.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The Sagarmala Programme focuses on port-led development, improving port infrastructure, connectivity, logistics and coastal economic activity.\nStatement 2 is correct: Improvements in coastal shipping, inland waterways and port performance reflect the broader objectives of Sagarmala.\nStatement 3 is correct in substance: Sagarmala 2.0 builds upon the earlier port-led development approach and gives greater emphasis to modernisation, innovation and India's future maritime ambitions.\nHowever, Statement 3 in the relationship question is incorrect because Statement I does not contradict Statement III. Traditional infrastructure development and maritime innovation can complement each other.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (b) 1 and 2.","revisionPoints":"Sagarmala Programme → port-led development.\nFocus areas include:\nPort modernisation\nPort connectivity\nPort-linked industrialisation\nCoastal community development\nAims to reduce logistics costs and improve logistics efficiency.\nPromotes coastal shipping and inland waterways.\nImportant connection: Ports → Connectivity → Logistics → Industry → Economic growth.\nSagarmala 2.0 → modernisation, innovation and maritime development.\nConnect with Atmanirbhar Bharat and Viksit Bharat 2047.\nUPSC may ask about Sagarmala through objectives, components, ports, coastal economic zones and multimodal connectivity."},
{"no":36,"text":"Consider the following statements about Rhynchostylis retusa (Foxtail Orchid):\n1. It is an epiphytic orchid.\n2. The species is endemic to North-east India.\n3. It is the State flower of Arunachal Pradesh and Assam.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environment & Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Rhynchostylis retusa, commonly known as the Foxtail Orchid, is an epiphytic orchid. It grows on other plants for physical support but does not derive its nutrients parasitically from them.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: The Foxtail Orchid is not endemic to North-east India. Its distribution extends beyond the region into other parts of South and Southeast Asia.\nStatement 3 is correct: The Foxtail Orchid is the State flower of Arunachal Pradesh and Assam.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 3 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Rhynchostylis retusa → Foxtail Orchid.\nIt is an epiphytic orchid.\nIt is found in India and other parts of South/Southeast Asia.\nState flower of Assam → Foxtail Orchid.\nState flower of Arunachal Pradesh → Foxtail Orchid.\nImportant distinction: Epiphyte ≠ parasite.\nEpiphytes use another plant mainly for physical support.\nOrchids belong to the family Orchidaceae.\nUPSC may ask about state symbols, endemic species, orchids and ecological adaptations."},
{"no":37,"text":"Which one of the following statements with regard to the Moidams, built by the Tai-Ahom kingdom and inscribed as a World Heritage Site by UNESCO, is/are correct?\n1. They acted as army fortresses.\n2. They were recreation centres of the Royals and Nobles.\n3. They were burial grounds of the Royals and Nobles.\n4. They were battle drill centres of the Royals and Nobles.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3","C":"3 only","D":"2 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Art & Culture","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The Moidams are burial mounds associated with the Tai-Ahom community and Ahom royalty of Assam.\nThey served as sacred burial grounds for Ahom kings, queens and members of the royal family and nobility.\nThey were not military fortresses, recreation centres or battle-drill centres.\nTherefore, Statement 3 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"Moidams → Assam.\nAssociated with the Tai-Ahom civilisation.\nFunction → burial grounds of Ahom royalty and nobility.\nLocated in the Charaideo region.\nCharaideo is regarded as the first capital of the Ahom kingdom.\nMoidams reflect the funerary tradition, beliefs and social hierarchy of the Ahom society.\nMoidams of the Ahom Dynasty → UNESCO World Heritage Site.\nUPSC may connect Moidams with Ahom history, Assam, funerary architecture and UNESCO heritage sites."},
{"no":38,"text":"At the United Nations Ocean Conference (UNOC) held in June 2025 in France, the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) of the United Nations demonstrated its leading voice on marine and ocean issues, especially on sustainable fisheries and aquaculture for resilient livelihood and \"Blue Transformation\".\nWhich of the following combinations about the \"Four Betters\" proposed by FAO for \"Blue Transformation\" is correct?\nOptions:","A":"Better production, better nutrition, better environment and better ocean","B":"Better production, better nutrition, better environment and better life","C":"Better coral reefs, better nutrition, better environment and better life","D":"Better estuaries, better nutrition, better environment and better mangrove vegetation","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environment & Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The FAO's Blue Transformation approach focuses on transforming aquatic food systems so that they become more productive, sustainable, resilient and inclusive.\nThe FAO's Four Betters framework consists of:\nBetter Production\nBetter Nutrition\nBetter Environment\nBetter Life\nTherefore, the correct answer is (b).","revisionPoints":"Blue Transformation → FAO.\nFocuses on sustainable and resilient aquatic food systems.\nIncludes fisheries and aquaculture.\nFour Betters:\nBetter Production\nBetter Nutrition\nBetter Environment\nBetter Life\nAquatic food systems contribute to food security and nutrition.\nSustainable fisheries help maintain marine biodiversity and ecosystem health.\nAquaculture → farming of aquatic organisms such as fish, shellfish and aquatic plants.\nUPSC may connect Blue Transformation with SDGs, food security, fisheries, aquaculture, oceans and livelihoods."},
{"no":39,"text":"Which of the following statements with reference to Lake Turkana is/are correct?\n1. It is the largest desert lake in the world.\n2. The lake is situated in South Sudan along the eastern fringe of the Sahara Desert.\n3. The lake is listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site and is also referred to as the \"Jade Sea\".\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geography","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Lake Turkana is widely recognised as the world's largest desert lake.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: Lake Turkana is located primarily in northern Kenya, not South Sudan. It lies in the East African Rift system.\nStatement 3 is correct: Lake Turkana is associated with the UNESCO World Heritage Site comprising the Lake Turkana National Parks, and the lake is popularly known as the \"Jade Sea\" because of its characteristic greenish colour.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 3 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Lake Turkana → Kenya.\nLocated in the East African Rift system.\nCommonly known as the Jade Sea.\nRecognised as the world's largest desert lake.\nLake Turkana National Parks are associated with a UNESCO World Heritage Site.\nThe lake is important for biodiversity and palaeoanthropological discoveries.\nThe region is associated with important evidence concerning human evolution.\nUPSC may ask about African lakes through location, geological setting, climatic zone and international significance.\nImportant African lakes to revise: Lake Turkana, Lake Victoria, Lake Tanganyika and Lake Malawi."},
{"no":40,"text":"Which one of the following is the first Plan Vivo certified Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation (REDD+) project in India?\nOptions:","A":"Uttarakhand REDD+ project","B":"ICFRE-ICIMOD Transboundary REDD+ project in North-Eastern Himalayas","C":"Khasi Hills Community REDD+ project","D":"Sikkim Mamley Kamrang Community REDD+ project","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environment & Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The Khasi Hills Community REDD+ Project in Meghalaya is recognised as the first Plan Vivo-certified REDD+ project in India.\nThe project focuses on community-based forest conservation, reducing deforestation and forest degradation while generating benefits for local communities.\nThe project demonstrates how forest conservation, carbon finance and community participation can be combined to address climate change and support local livelihoods.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (c) Khasi Hills Community REDD+ project.","revisionPoints":"Khasi Hills Community REDD+ Project → Meghalaya.\nIt is associated with community-based forest conservation.\nREDD+ → Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation.\nREDD+ aims to reduce emissions associated with deforestation and forest degradation.\nIt also promotes conservation, sustainable forest management and enhancement of forest carbon stocks.\nPlan Vivo is associated with standards for community-based land-use and ecosystem projects.\nImportant connection: Forest conservation → Carbon sequestration → Climate mitigation → Community livelihoods.\nUPSC may connect REDD+ with carbon markets, forests, climate finance, indigenous/local communities and biodiversity conservation.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (c) Khasi Hills Community REDD+ project."},
{"no":41,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to genetic medicines is/are correct?\n1. Genetic medicines correct/compensate for the faulty genes responsible for disease.\n2. Engineered viruses and lipid nanoparticles are used as carriers of the genetic medicine.\n3. Genetic medicines alter the entire DNA sequence.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Genetic medicines are designed to treat diseases by correcting, replacing, modifying or compensating for faulty genetic material or its effects.\nStatement 2 is correct: Genetic medicines can use delivery systems such as engineered viruses (viral vectors) and lipid nanoparticles to transport genetic material into target cells.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Genetic medicines do not necessarily alter the entire DNA sequence of a person. Depending on the therapy, they may replace a faulty gene, introduce genetic material, modify gene expression or edit a specific part of the genome.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Genetic medicine → treatment based on genetic material or modification of genetic function.\nMajor approaches include gene replacement, gene addition, gene editing and gene silencing.\nViral vectors → engineered viruses used to deliver genetic material.\nLipid nanoparticles → non-viral delivery systems used to transport genetic material into cells.\nCRISPR-Cas systems → important gene-editing technology.\nGenetic medicines generally target specific genes, cells or biological pathways, not the entire DNA sequence.\nUPSC may connect genetic medicines with gene therapy, genome editing, CRISPR, viral vectors and biotechnology."},
{"no":42,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to Large Language Models (LLMs) used in machine learning is/are correct?\n1. LLMs assign probabilities to the next possible words and then pick the one with the highest probability.\n2. LLMs process data through mathematical optimization to minimise prediction errors.\n3. LLMs produce unbiased outputs.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: LLMs estimate the probability distribution of possible next tokens based on the preceding context. During generation, a model may select a high-probability token, although it does not always simply choose the single highest-probability token because sampling methods can also be used.\nStatement 2 is correct: During training, LLMs use mathematical optimisation to minimise a loss function representing prediction errors. Model parameters are adjusted repeatedly to improve predictions.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: LLMs do not necessarily produce unbiased outputs. Their outputs can reflect biases present in training data, model design or prompting.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"LLM → Large Language Model.\nLLMs are a type of machine-learning model designed to process and generate language.\nLLMs predict tokens, not necessarily complete words.\nTraining involves loss functions and mathematical optimisation.\nNext-token prediction is a fundamental mechanism in many language models.\nModel outputs can contain bias, errors and hallucinations.\nImportant concepts: training data, parameters, tokens, neural networks, optimisation and inference.\nUPSC may connect AI with machine learning, deep learning, LLMs, generative AI and responsible AI."},
{"no":43,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to stealth technology is/are correct?\n1. Stealth objects have a very small radar cross-section and are coated with Radar Absorbing Material.\n2. Stealth objects can be detected using specific frequencies.\n3. Stealth objects are coated with metamaterials to increase the scattering of electromagnetic radiation.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct:Stealth technology is used to make an aircraft, ship, missile, etc. difficult to detect. Stealth objects are designed to have a small Radar Cross-Section (RCS). They may use Radar Absorbing Materials (RAM) to absorb some of the radar energy.Their shape is also specially designed to deflect radar waves away from the radar receiver.\nStatement 2 is correct: Stealth technology does not make an object completely invisible to radar. Detection can be possible using appropriate frequencies, radar techniques and other sensing methods.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: The key word is \"increase scattering.\" Stealth technology aims to reduce or control the reflection/scattering of electromagnetic waves toward the detector.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 2 only  correct.","revisionPoints":"Stealth technology → Makes an object difficult to detect\nMain objective → Reduce detectability\nRadar Cross-Section (RCS) → Measure of how detectable an object is by radar\nStealth object → Has a low/small RCS\nRAM → Radar Absorbing Material\nRAM → Absorbs radar energy\nStealth design → Deflects radar waves away from the receiver\nStealth ≠ Complete invisibility\nDifferent frequencies → Can help in detecting stealth objects\nStealth technology is used in → Aircraft, ships, missiles, drones, etc.\nMetamaterials → Can be engineered to control electromagnetic waves\nImportant trap → Stealth aims to reduce/control scattering, NOT increase it"},
{"no":44,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to Black Boxes used in modern aircraft is/are correct?\n1. They carry a beacon emitting red light pulses to facilitate underwater detection.\n2. They record both the cockpit voice and flight data.\n3. Their memory units are made using either stainless steel or titanium.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement I is incorrect: Aircraft black boxes have an Underwater Locator Beacon (ULB). It helps locate the black box when an aircraft crashes into water. But it does not emit red light pulses. It sends acoustic/sonar signals (pings) underwater.\nStatement II is correct:CVR (Cockpit Voice Recorder) → records sounds/voices in the cockpit. FDR (Flight Data Recorder) → records flight information such as altitude, speed, direction, etc.\nStatement III is correct: The memory unit is designed to survive extreme conditions. It is protected by strong materials such as stainless steel or titanium.","revisionPoints":"Black box → Aircraft flight recorder\nMain purpose → Records important information about the flight\nCVR → Cockpit Voice Recorder\nCVR → Records cockpit conversations and sounds\nFDR → Flight Data Recorder\nFDR → Records flight parameters\nULB → Underwater Locator Beacon\nULB → Helps locate the recorder underwater\nULB signal → Acoustic/sonar \"pings\", NOT red light\nMemory unit → Protected using strong materials such as titanium/stainless steel\nBlack box is designed to survive → Fire, impact, extreme temperature and pressure\nBlack box colour → Usually bright orange, not actually black"},
{"no":45,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to Green Hydrogen is/are correct?\n1. It is decarbonized hydrogen obtained from natural gas reforming combined with carbon capture and storage (CCS).\n2. It is produced using electrolysis of water with electricity generated by renewable energy.\n3. National Green Hydrogen Mission of India aims for abatement of nearly 50 MMT of annual greenhouse gas emissions by 2030.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Hydrogen produced from natural gas reforming combined with carbon capture and storage is generally known as blue hydrogen, not green hydrogen.\nStatement 2 is correct: Green hydrogen is produced by the electrolysis of water using electricity generated from renewable energy sources such as solar and wind power.\nStatement 3 is correct: India's National Green Hydrogen Mission aims to help reduce greenhouse gas emissions by approximately 50 million metric tonnes annually by 2030.\nTherefore, Statements 2 and 3 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Green hydrogen → electrolysis of water using renewable electricity.\nBlue hydrogen → fossil-fuel-based hydrogen + carbon capture and storage.\nGreen hydrogen can help decarbonise sectors that are difficult to electrify directly.\nMajor potential applications include fertilisers, refining, steel and heavy transport.\nNational Green Hydrogen Mission → India.\nMission target → at least 5 MMT annual green hydrogen production capacity by 2030.\nAssociated renewable energy capacity target → about 125 GW.\nGreen hydrogen is important for India's energy transition and decarbonisation.\nUPSC may compare grey, blue and green hydrogen."},
{"no":46,"text":"Consider the following statements with regard to involvement of private entities in India's space programme:\n1. The Indian National Space Promotion and Authorisation Centre (IN-SPACe) is an autonomous agency formed to facilitate participation of private entities.\n2. Agnikul Cosmos launched the world's first flight using a 3D-printed rocket engine.\n3. Skyroot Aerospace has developed liquid fuel for GSLV.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: IN-SPACe was established as an autonomous agency under the Department of Space to promote, facilitate and regulate the participation of private entities in India's space sector.\nStatement 2 is correct: Agnikul Cosmos developed a rocket engine using 3D-printing technology and achieved a significant milestone with its launch vehicle technology.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Skyroot Aerospace is a private Indian space company that has developed launch vehicles and rocket engines, but it has not developed the liquid fuel for the GSLV. GSLV is an ISRO launch vehicle.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"IN-SPACe → Indian National Space Promotion and Authorisation Centre.\nFacilitates participation of private sector entities in India's space activities.\nIN-SPACe works under the Department of Space.\nAgnikul Cosmos → private Indian space company.\nAgnikul developed 3D-printed rocket-engine technology.\nSkyroot Aerospace → private Indian space company.\nISRO → Indian Space Research Organisation.\nIndia's space-sector reforms aim to increase private-sector participation and innovation.\nImportant organisations: ISRO, IN-SPACe, NSIL.\nUPSC may ask about space-sector reforms, private companies, launch vehicles and space institutions."},
{"no":47,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to drone swarms is/are correct?\n1. They use Terahertz band of frequency to communicate with the command centre.\n2. Individual drones in the swarm can communicate with other drones in the swarm.\n3. GPS Spoofing is a commonly used technique to counter drone swarm attack.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: They can use technologies such as radio frequencies, satellite communication, Wi-Fi and other wireless systems.Terahertz frequency is not the standard/common communication method for drone swarms.\nStatement 2 is correct: A key feature of a drone swarm is communication between individual drones. This allows drones to coordinate movement, share information and perform tasks collectively.\nStatement 3 is correct: GPS spoofing means sending false GPS signals to a drone. It can therefore be used as one of the techniques in counter-drone operations.\nTherefore, Statements 2 and 3 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Drone swarm → Group of drones working together\nIndividual drones can communicate → Yes\nDrone-to-drone communication → Helps coordination\nSwarm can share information → Yes\nMain advantage → Can perform tasks collectively\nGPS Spoofing → Sending false GPS signals\nGPS Spoofing → Can mislead a drone about its location\nGPS Spoofing → One method of countering drones\nTerahertz communication → Not the standard/common communication method for drone swarms"},
{"no":48,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to GenomeIndia Project is/are correct?\n1. It is a part of the Human Genome Project.\n2. The project is funded by the Department of Biotechnology (DBT), Government of India.\n3. Its primary aim is to build a catalogue of genetic diversity of the Indian population.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement I is incorrect: The Human Genome Project (HGP) was an international scientific project that aimed to map and understand the human genome. The GenomeIndia Project is a separate Indian project. It is not a part of the Human Genome Project.\nStatement II is correct : The GenomeIndia Project (GIP) is funded by the Department of Biotechnology (DBT). It involves several Indian research institutions working together.\nStatement III is correct: The main objective is to understand the genetic diversity of India's population.","revisionPoints":"GenomeIndia Project → Indian genomics research initiative\nFunded by → Department of Biotechnology (DBT)\nMain aim → Catalogue India's genetic diversity\nFocus → Genetic variations among Indian populations\nUseful for → Understanding genetic diseases\nPotential application → Precision/Personalised medicine\nGenomeIndia Project ≠ Human Genome Project\nHuman Genome Project → International project\nGenomeIndia Project → Indian initiative"},
{"no":49,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to the National Quantum Mission (NQM) is/are correct?\n1. It aims at developing intermediate-scale quantum computers with 50–1000 physical qubits.\n2. Its implementation includes setting up four Thematic Hubs (T-Hubs) in academic and national R&amp;D institutes across India.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The National Quantum Mission (NQM) aims to develop quantum computers in India. Its target is to develop quantum computers with 50–1000 physical qubits.\nStatement 2 is correct: The National Quantum Mission includes the establishment of four Thematic Hubs focusing on key areas of quantum technology.\nThese areas include Quantum Computing, Quantum Communication, Quantum Sensing &amp; Metrology, and Quantum Materials &amp; Devices.\nTherefore, Both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"NQM → India's mission for quantum technology\nApproved → 2023\nImplemented by → Department of Science &amp; Technology (DST)\nTarget period → 2023–24 to 2030–31\nQuantum computer target → 50–1000 physical qubits\nFour Thematic Hubs → Yes\nT-Hubs focus on → Quantum Computing, Communication, Sensing &amp; Metrology, Materials &amp; Devices\nMain areas → Quantum Computing, Communication, Sensing, Materials\nObjective → Build India's quantum technology ecosystem\nImportant goal → Develop indigenous quantum technologies"},
{"no":50,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to India's Deep Ocean Mission is/are correct?\n1. It was launched by the Ministry of Ports, Shipping and Waterways, Government of India.\n2. Matsya-6000 has been designed to carry 3 people for deep sea exploration.\n3. Samudrayaan is a project under this mission.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:\nOptions:","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The Deep Ocean Mission is implemented under the Ministry of Earth Sciences (MoES), not the Ministry of Ports, Shipping and Waterways.\nStatement 2 is correct: Matsya-6000 is a deep-sea manned submersible designed to carry three people to explore the deep ocean.\nStatement 3 is correct: Samudrayaan is India's programme for sending humans into the deep ocean and is a component of the Deep Ocean Mission.\nTherefore, Statements 2 and 3 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Deep Ocean Mission → Ministry of Earth Sciences.\nSamudrayaan → India's manned deep-ocean exploration programme.\nMatsya-6000 → manned submersible.\nMatsya-6000 is designed to carry three people.\nDesigned for deep-sea exploration at depths of around 6,000 metres.\nMission objectives include deep-sea exploration, marine biodiversity, mineral resources and ocean technology.\nDeep Ocean Mission is important for India's Blue Economy.\nImportant distinction:\nDeep Ocean Mission → Ministry of Earth Sciences\nSagarmala → Ministry of Ports, Shipping and Waterways\nUPSC may connect deep-ocean exploration with Blue Economy, polymetallic nodules, marine biodiversity, submersibles and ocean resources."},
{"no":51,"text":"Mr. X, a senior officer, was overseeing a critical vaccination programme during a pandemic. He found that a private service provider responsible for vaccine distribution was compromising on quality to make profits. Despite immense pressure to manage the issue due to vested interests, he raised his voice based on the principles of public administration which he learnt during various training programmes attended across his career. He reported the issue to the appropriate vigilance authority and halted the contract to ensure citizen welfare.\nWhich one among the following principles of public administration was most strongly demonstrated by Mr. X's actions?","A":"Esprit de corps","B":"Equity","C":"Accountability","D":"Delegation","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Accountability means being answerable for decisions, actions and the proper use of public resources.\nMr. X identified the quality compromise, reported it to the vigilance authority and stopped the contract despite pressure from vested interests.\nHis actions demonstrate responsibility towards public welfare and willingness to subject administrative decisions to oversight.\nEsprit de corps refers to team spirit, equity to fairness, and delegation to assigning authority to others.\nTherefore, Accountability is the strongest principle demonstrated.","revisionPoints":"Accountability = answerability + responsibility + oversight.\nPublic officials must protect public interest, even when facing external or institutional pressure.\nVigilance mechanisms are important instruments of administrative accountability.\nQuick recall: Reporting wrongdoing and taking corrective action → Accountability."},
{"no":52,"text":"In a multi-ethnic district where both economic competition and historical grievances frequently led to community tensions, a flashpoint has arisen with a Government decision to allocate land for a waste management facility near a tribal hamlet. The tribal community claimed that the land was sacred and critical to its cultural identity, while urban residents and local industries supported the project because of serious solid-waste and public-health concerns. The conflict escalated through protests, road blockades, social-media campaigns and allegations of police excesses.\nAs a responsible Government official, you are tasked with resolving the situation through mediation, ensuring a sustainable outcome that balances environmental needs, tribal rights and urban public health.\nConsider the following statements:\n1. A successful conflict resolution process must begin by acknowledging the cultural concerns of the protesting tribal community before discussing technical alternatives.\n2. The Government should move ahead with the project without delay to address urban health concerns, which outweigh the sentiments of a small group.\n3. Creating a multi-stakeholder dialogue platform including tribal leaders, environmental experts and municipal representatives can help build mutual understanding and de-escalate tensions.\n4. Conducting an independent Environmental and Social Impact Assessment (ESIA) and sharing findings transparently with both sides can facilitate evidence-based decision-making.\nWhich of the statements given above would contribute to the resolution process?","A":"1, 3 and 4 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Recognising cultural concerns is essential for building trust and ensuring that the affected community feels heard.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: Proceeding without meaningful consultation would aggravate the conflict and disregard legitimate tribal and cultural concerns.\nStatement 3 is correct: Multi-stakeholder dialogue allows different interests to be represented and encourages negotiated solutions.\nStatement 4 is correct: An independent ESIA provides objective information about environmental and social consequences and supports evidence-based decision-making.\nHence, 1, 3 and 4 only are appropriate.","revisionPoints":"Effective conflict resolution requires consultation, inclusion, dialogue and evidence.\nAvoid majoritarian or unilateral decision-making in sensitive conflicts.\nMulti-stakeholder platforms can include:\naffected communities\ngovernment authorities\nexperts\ncivil society\nindustry representatives\nESIA helps assess both environmental and social consequences.\nQuick recall: Conflict resolution → Acknowledge + Dialogue + Assess + Decide transparently."},
{"no":53,"text":"Ms. X is a mid-level civil service official working in the urban development department of a major city. She was involved in approving a contract for a public infrastructure project. During the approval process, she received confidential information indicating that one of the shortlisted contractors had a history of poor workmanship and allegations of corruption in other cities, although nothing had been legally proven. The Head of Department advised her not to disclose the information because it could delay the project and damage the city's reputation. However, Ms. X believed that withholding such information compromised transparency and public trust.\nWhat amongst the following should Ms. X do now?\n1. Immediately disclose the information to the project committee and the public.\n2. Recommend removing the contractor from the shortlist to protect the project's integrity.\n3. Propose a limited disclosure to an oversight committee while keeping the information confidential from the public for the time being.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The information is confidential. The allegations against the contractor have not been legally proven.\nStatement 2 is incorrect:The information contains allegations, but nothing has been legally established.\nMs. X should not make a final decision to remove the contractor solely on the basis of unverified allegations.\nInstead, the matter should be properly examined by an authorised oversight body.\nStatement 3 is correct : This is the best balanced approach.\nTaken together, the three actions promote transparency, integrity, accountability and responsible handling of confidential information.\nTherefore, the correct answer is 3 only.","revisionPoints":"Public interest → Primary consideration\nTransparency → Important for good governance\nConfidentiality → Should be maintained when information is sensitive\nUnverified allegations → Should not be treated as proven facts\nDue process → Follow proper investigation and procedure\nAccountability → Report serious concerns to the appropriate authority\nWhistleblowing → Should follow lawful and authorised channels where possible\nPublic disclosure → Should be made carefully when legally and ethically justified\nDecision-making → Balance transparency, confidentiality and fairness"},
{"no":54,"text":"'X' was addressing a seminar on the meaning of the term \"law\" as provided under Article 13, Part III of the Constitution of India. 'X' explained that the meaning of \"law\" in the Constitution of India was very comprehensive. It included ordinances, orders and even rules and regulations. 'Y' pointed out that the term \"law\" in Article 13 also included custom or usage having in the territory of India the force of law, to which 'X' was not convinced.\nBased on the above, select the correct conclusion from the options given below:","A":"'X' is correct in the interpretation of law, including the view on non-inclusion of custom.","B":"The view of 'Y' that 'law' included custom is not correct.","C":"The views of both 'X' and 'Y' are correct.","D":"The view of only 'Y' is correct.","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Article 13(3)(a) gives a broad definition of \"law\".\nIt includes an Ordinance, order, bye-law, rule, regulation, notification, custom or usage having the force of law in India.\nTherefore, Y is correct in stating that legally enforceable custom or usage is included within the meaning of law under Article 13.\nX's interpretation is incomplete because he rejects the inclusion of custom or usage.\nHence, only Y's view is correct.","revisionPoints":"Article 13(3)(a) → broad definition of \"law\".\nIncludes:\nOrdinance\nOrder\nBye-law\nRule\nRegulation\nNotification\nCustom\nUsage having force of law\nArticle 13 is closely associated with Fundamental Rights and judicial review.\nQuick recall: Article 13 → Laws inconsistent with Fundamental Rights are void."},
{"no":55,"text":"Consider the following statements with reference to the Constitution of India:\n1. There is no Article in the Constitution of India that specifies that the Constitution of India will be officially called the 'Constitution of India'.\n2. There is no Article in the Constitution of India that specifies that the Indian Independence Act, 1947 and the Government of India Act, 1935 stand repealed.\n3. There is no Article in the Constitution of India that mentions 26th January, 1950 as the date of the commencement of the Constitution of India.\nWhich one of the following conclusions based on the above statements is correct?","A":"All three statements are correct.","B":"There is no correct statement.","C":"There are two correct statements that include statement 3.","D":"There is only one correct statement.","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"All three statements are incorrect:\nStatement 1 is incorrect: Article 393 provides the short title: \"This Constitution may be called the Constitution of India.\"\nStatement 2 is incorrect: Article 395 expressly repeals the Indian Independence Act, 1947 and the Government of India Act, 1935.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Article 394 expressly refers to 26 January 1950 as the commencement date for the remaining provisions of the Constitution.\nTherefore, there is no correct statement among the three.","revisionPoints":"Article 393 → Short title: Constitution of India.\nArticle 394 → Commencement of constitutional provisions.\nArticle 395 → Repeals the Indian Independence Act, 1947 and Government of India Act, 1935.\n26 November 1949 → Constitution adopted/enacted.\n26 January 1950 → Constitution came into force substantially.\nQuick recall: 393 = Name, 394 = Commencement, 395 = Repeal."},
{"no":56,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to the persons with disabilities in India is/are correct?\n1. The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, an Act passed by the Parliament of India in 2018, mandates reservation in education and employment, places a legal duty on Governments to ensure accessibility and non-discrimination.\n2. The Sugamya Bharat Abhiyan focuses on achieving universal accessibility for Persons with Disabilities across three key domains — built infrastructure, transport systems and information and communication technology.\n3. The National Divyangjan Finance and Development Corporation (NDFDC) is a public sector organisation set up by the Ministry of Corporate Affairs as a not-for-profit company to promote entrepreneurship among Persons with Disabilities (PwDs).\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"1 only","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act was enacted in 2016, not 2018.\nStatement 2 is correct: The Sugamya Bharat Abhiyan (Accessible India Campaign) focuses on accessibility in three major domains:\nBuilt environment\nTransportation\nInformation and Communication Technology\nStatement 3 is incorrect: NDFDC functions under the Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities, Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment, not the Ministry of Corporate Affairs.\nTherefore, only Statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"RPwD Act → 2016\nSugamya Bharat Abhiyan → 2015\nThree accessibility domains:\nBuilt environment\nTransport\nICT\nNDFDC → Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment\nUPSC trap: Pay attention to incorrect ministry and year."},
{"no":57,"text":"Consider the following statements about the provisions pertaining to the Scheduled Castes and the Scheduled Tribes in India:\n1. Provisions regarding the administration of the Tribal Areas in the States of Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram are given in the Fifth Schedule of the Constitution of India.\n2. Some tribes of India are entitled to exemption from paying Income Tax on certain incomes.\n3. The Constitution of India provides for reservation of seats in Panchayats for women belonging to the Scheduled Castes and the Scheduled Tribes.\nWhich one of the following conclusions based on the above statements is correct?","A":"There are two correct statements, that include statement 2.","B":"There are two correct statements, that are statements 1 and 3.","C":"There is only one correct statement.","D":"All three statements are correct.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Tribal Areas in Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram are governed under the Sixth Schedule, not the Fifth Schedule.\nStatement 2 is correct: Certain members of Scheduled Tribes are eligible for income-tax exemptions on specified incomes under the Income-tax Act.\nStatement 3 is correct: Article 243D provides reservation for SCs and STs in Panchayats, including reservation of seats for women belonging to these communities.\nThus, Statements 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Fifth Schedule → Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes in specified States.\nSixth Schedule → Tribal Areas of:\nAssam\nMeghalaya\nTripura\nMizoram\nArticle 243D → Reservation in Panchayats.\nRemember the distinction: Fifth Schedule ≠ Sixth Schedule."},
{"no":58,"text":"Consider the following statements in respect of questions asked by the Members in the Parliament of India:\n1. Unstarred questions are those to which a Member desires an oral answer in the House.\n2. Starred questions are those to which a Member desires a written answer.\n3. No supplementary question can be asked on an unstarred question.\nWhich one of the following conclusions based on the above statements is correct?","A":"All the three statements are correct.","B":"There are two correct statements, that include statement 2.","C":"There is only one correct statement.","D":"There is no correct statement.","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: An unstarred question requires a written answer, not an oral answer.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: A starred question requires an oral answer, and it is marked with an asterisk.\nStatement 3 is correct: Supplementary questions cannot be asked on an unstarred question because its answer is given in writing.\nTherefore, only Statement 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Starred Question → Oral answer → Supplementary questions allowed.\nUnstarred Question → Written answer → No supplementary questions.\nStarred questions are identified by an asterisk (*).\nQuick recall: Starred = Speaking; Unstarred = Written."},
{"no":59,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Committee on the Welfare of Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes of the Parliament of India:\n1. Although members of this Committee are elected from both Houses of Parliament, the Chairperson of this Committee is appointed by the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha.\n2. Twenty members are elected by the Rajya Sabha and ten members by the Lok Sabha.\n3. No Minister, except for the Union Minister of Social Justice and Empowerment, is eligible to be a member of this Committee.\n4. Members are elected for a fixed term of two years from the date they enter their office.\nWhich one of the following conclusions based on the above statements is correct?","A":"There are four correct statements.","B":"There is only one correct statement, that is statement 2.","C":"There are two correct statements, that include statement 1.","D":"There is no correct statement.","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"All four statements are incorrect:\nStatement 1 is incorrect: The Chairperson is appointed by the Speaker of the Lok Sabha, not the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: The Committee consists of 20 members from the Lok Sabha and 10 from the Rajya Sabha, not the reverse.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: No Minister is eligible to be a member of the Committee. If an existing member becomes a Minister, they cease to be a member.\nStatement 4 is incorrect: The Committee's term does not exceed one year, not two years.\nTherefore, there is no correct statement.","revisionPoints":"Committee on Welfare of SCs and STs:\n30 members\n20 Lok Sabha + 10 Rajya Sabha\nChairperson appointed by Speaker of Lok Sabha\nNo Minister can be a member\nTerm → One year\nQuick recall: 30 = 20 LS + 10 RS; 1-year term."},
{"no":60,"text":"Consider the following statements about Mission Sudarshan Chakra of India:\n1. It aims to enhance India's air defence, ballistic missile defence and aerial offensive capabilities.\n2. This Mission is being designed to enhance rapid, precise, and powerful defence responses, reinforcing India's strategic autonomy.\n3. One of the aims of this Mission is to cover all public places of India by an expanded nationwide security shield by 2035.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 only","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Mission Sudarshan Chakra is intended to strengthen India's multi-domain air and missile defence capabilities as well as associated offensive capabilities.\nStatement 2 is correct: The mission seeks rapid, precise and powerful defence responses while strengthening India's strategic autonomy and indigenous defence capabilities.\nStatement 3 is correct: The mission envisages an expanded nationwide security shield covering public places as part of its long-term security objective by 2035.\nTherefore, all three statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Mission Sudarshan Chakra → national security and defence shield.\nKey areas:\nAir defence\nBallistic missile defence\nAerial offensive capabilities\nRapid and precise response\nStrategic autonomy\nTarget horizon mentioned → 2035.\nQuick recall: Sudarshan Chakra → multi-domain defence + nationwide security shield."},
{"no":61,"text":"Consider the following statements about river bridges connecting India with neighbouring countries:\n1. ‘Maitri Setu’, built over Feni river, connects Ramgarh in India with Sabroom in Bangladesh.\n2. Jhulaghat suspension bridge connects India with Myanmar.\n3. Mechi bridge and its approaches connect Panitanki Bypass in India with Kakarvitta in Nepal.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 only","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Geography","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Maitri Setu is built over the Feni River and connects Sabroom in Tripura, India, with Ramgarh in Bangladesh. The statement reverses the locations.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: The Jhulaghat suspension bridge connects India and Nepal, not India and Myanmar. It spans the Mahakali/Sharda River and links Uttarakhand with Nepal.\nStatement 3 is correct: The Mechi Bridge connects Panitanki Bypass in West Bengal, India, with Kakarvitta in Nepal.\nTherefore, only Statement 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Maitri Setu → Sabroom, India ↔ Ramgarh, Bangladesh → Feni River.\nJhulaghat Bridge → India ↔ Nepal.\nMechi Bridge → Panitanki, India ↔ Kakarvitta, Nepal.\nUPSC often tests reversal of places and countries in international-border questions."},
{"no":62,"text":"Which of the following statements about a Zero First Information Report (Zero FIR) under the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS), 2023 is/are correct?\n1. A Zero FIR can be lodged at a police station, even though the place of commission of a cognizable offence is outside the territorial jurisdiction of that police station.\n2. The Officer-in-Charge of the police station where a Zero FIR has been lodged may, with the permission of the competent authority, initiate a preliminary enquiry.\n3. Under Zero FIR, it is obligatory for the informant to furnish information electronically.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 only","D":"2 only","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 reflects the basic concept of a Zero FIR: an FIR can be registered irrespective of territorial jurisdiction and subsequently transferred to the police station having jurisdiction.\nStatement 2 relates to preliminary enquiry under BNSS. The provision for preliminary enquiry is subject to statutory conditions and permission of the competent senior police officer; it is not an unrestricted power applicable to every Zero FIR.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Electronic communication is permitted under the BNSS framework, but furnishing information electronically is not an obligatory condition for lodging a Zero FIR.\nThe wording of the question created legal/interpretational ambiguity, and UPSC subsequently removed it.","revisionPoints":"Zero FIR → FIR can be registered irrespective of territorial jurisdiction.\nIt is subsequently transferred to the police station having appropriate territorial jurisdiction.\nElectronic communication ≠ mandatory electronic FIR in every case.\nBNSS provisions on preliminary enquiry must be read with the nature and punishment of the cognizable offence.\nExam caution: Q62 was a disputed/withdrawn question."},
{"no":63,"text":"With reference to the organisations under the Government of India, consider the following details:\n1. Sl. No.\n2. Organisation\n3. Function\n4. Controlling Union Ministry\n5. 1\n6. Central Economic Intelligence Bureau (CEIB)\n7. To coordinate between various law enforcement agencies\n8. Ministry of Home Affairs\n9. 2\n10. Serious Fraud Investigation Office (SFIO)\n11. To investigate complex corporate frauds\n12. Ministry of Finance\n13. 3\n14. Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI)\n15. To preserve values in public life and ensure the health of the national economy\n16. Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pension\nIn how many of the above rows are the given details correctly matched?","A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"None","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Row 1 is incorrect: CEIB performs economic-intelligence coordination functions, but it is under the Ministry of Finance, not the Ministry of Home Affairs.\nRow 2 is incorrect: SFIO investigates complex corporate frauds, but it functions under the Ministry of Corporate Affairs, not the Ministry of Finance.\nRow 3 is correct: CBI is under the administrative control of the Department of Personnel and Training, Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions. The stated description is associated with its role in tackling corruption and economic offences.\nHence, only one row is correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"CEIB → Ministry of Finance → economic intelligence.\nSFIO → Ministry of Corporate Affairs → complex corporate fraud.\nCBI → DoPT → Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions.\nUPSC trap: The function may be correct while the controlling ministry is wrong."},
{"no":64,"text":"Which of the following international conventions have not been ratified by India?\n1. Employment Policy Convention\n2. Abolition of Forced Labour Convention\n3. International Convention on the Protection of the Rights of All Migrant Workers and Members of Their Families\n4. Geneva Convention Relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War\n5. Convention on Reduction of Statelessness\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"2 and 4","B":"1 and 2","C":"3 and 4 only","D":"3, 4 and 5","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Relations","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The actual treaty-status position creates a problem with the options:\nEmployment Policy Convention (ILO Convention No. 122): India ratified it in 1998.\nAbolition of Forced Labour Convention (ILO Convention No. 105): India ratified it in 2000.\nMigrant Workers Convention, 1990: India has not ratified it.\nConvention on the Reduction of Statelessness, 1961: India has not ratified it.\nFourth Geneva Convention, 1949: India has ratified it.\nThus, the factually unratified conventions among the five are 3 and 5, but that combination is not provided in the options. Consequently, the question was withdrawn.","revisionPoints":"ILO Convention No. 122 → India ratified.\nILO Convention No. 105 → India ratified.\nMigrant Workers Convention, 1990 → Not ratified by India.\nConvention on Reduction of Statelessness, 1961 → Not ratified by India.\nFourth Geneva Convention, 1949 → India is a party.\nImportant UPSC lesson: Always distinguish the actual treaty status from a potentially defective answer option."},
{"no":65,"text":"Consider the following statements with respect to the AI Impact Summit, 2026 held in New Delhi:\n1. The Summit's intellectual framework was based on three foundational Sutras: People, Planning and Progress.\n2. The Preamble of the Summit stresses Democratising AI Resources, which acknowledges the Charter for Democratic Diffusion of AI as a binding framework to support locally relevant innovation and strengthen resilient AI ecosystems while respecting national laws.\n3. The New Delhi Declaration on AI Impact was structured around seven Chakras (Pillars), which included Access for Social Empowerment, AI for Science, and Secure and Trusted AI.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Current Affairs","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The three foundational Sutras were People, Planet and Progress, not People, Planning and Progress.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: The Charter for Democratic Diffusion of AI was a voluntary and non-binding framework, not a binding framework.\nStatement 3 is correct: The Summit's framework included seven Chakras, including areas such as Access for Social Empowerment, AI for Science and Safe/Secure and Trusted AI.\nTherefore, only Statement 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Three Sutras: People, Planet, Progress.\nSeven Chakras: thematic areas for AI cooperation.\nCharter for Democratic Diffusion of AI → voluntary/non-binding.\nKey themes include:\nHuman Capital\nInclusion for Social Empowerment\nSafe and Trusted AI\nAI for Science\nDemocratizing AI Resources\nEconomic Growth and Social Good\nResilience, Innovation and Efficiency\nQuick recall: 3 Ps → 7 Chakras."},
{"no":66,"text":"Which of the following connectivity projects is/are a part of cooperation between India and the ASEAN member countries?\n1. Kaladan Multi-Modal Transit Transport Project\n2. IMT Trilateral Highway\n3. Agartala–Akhaura Rail Line\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"2 only","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Relations","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The Kaladan project connects India with Myanmar, an ASEAN member, through multimodal sea, river and road connectivity.\nStatement 2 is correct: The India-Myanmar-Thailand Trilateral Highway connects India with Myanmar and Thailand, both ASEAN members.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: The Agartala–Akhaura Rail Line connects India and Bangladesh. Bangladesh is not an ASEAN member.\nHence, 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Kaladan Project → India–Myanmar.\nIMT Trilateral Highway → India–Myanmar–Thailand.\nAgartala–Akhaura → India–Bangladesh.\nASEAN has 10 member states.\nQuick recall: India-ASEAN connectivity → think Myanmar + Thailand."},
{"no":67,"text":"Match List I with List II and select the answer using the code given below.\nList I – Project Supported by India\nA. Mangdechhu Hydroelectric ProjectB. Restoration of Store PalaceC. District Hospital at DickoyaD. Institute of Security and Law Enforcement Studies\nList II – Country\nMaldives\nAfghanistan\nBhutan\nSri Lanka\nOptions:","A":"A-1 B-4 C-2 D-3","B":"A-3 B-2 C-4 D-1","C":"A-3 B-4 C-2 D-1","D":"A-1 B-2 C-4 D-3","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Relations","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The correct matching is:\nA. Mangdechhu Hydroelectric Project → 3. Bhutan\nB. Restoration of Store Palace → 2. Afghanistan\nC. District Hospital at Dickoya → 4. Sri Lanka\nD. Institute of Security and Law Enforcement Studies → 1. Maldives\nTherefore, the correct combination is A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1.","revisionPoints":"Mangdechhu Hydroelectric Project → Bhutan\nStore Palace → Afghanistan\nDickoya District Hospital → Sri Lanka\nInstitute of Security and Law Enforcement Studies → Maldives\nUPSC frequently tests India's development partnership and grant-in-aid projects with neighbouring countries."},
{"no":68,"text":"Which of the following items of defence hardware is/are manufactured in India?\n1. Su-30 MKI Fighter Jets\n2. T-90 MK-II Tanks\n3. Akula Class Submarine\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 2","B":"1 and 3","C":"1 only","D":"2 only","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Su-30 MKI: Manufactured in India by Hindustan Aeronautics Limited (HAL) under a licensed production and technology-transfer arrangement with Russia.\nT-90 tanks: T-90 tanks are manufactured in India under licensed production arrangements, including production at the Heavy Vehicles Factory in Avadi.\nAkula-class submarine: These nuclear-powered attack submarines were designed and built in Russia. India has operated/leased Akula-class submarines but does not manufacture them domestically.\nTherefore, 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Su-30 MKI → HAL → India\nT-90 → Indian licensed production → India\nAkula class → Russian origin → not manufactured in India\nImportant distinction:\nManufactured in India ≠ designed originally in India.\nLicensed production can still constitute domestic manufacturing."},
{"no":69,"text":"Consider the following statements about platforms for multilateral co-operation:\n1. The 'Colombo Process' is a regional consultative process in which member states take binding decisions by consensus.\n2. The 'Abu Dhabi Dialogue' is a voluntary non-binding consultative process among Asian countries of labour origin and destination to facilitate regional cooperation on contractual labour mobility.\n3. The 'Global Forum for Migration and Development', created upon the proposal of a former UN Secretary General, is a voluntary forum whose decisions are non-binding in nature.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"2 only","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Relations","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The Colombo Process is a regional consultative process, but its decisions are voluntary and non-binding, not binding.\nStatement 2 is correct: The Abu Dhabi Dialogue is a voluntary, non-binding consultative process involving Asian countries of labour origin and destination and focuses on contractual labour mobility.\nStatement 3 is correct: The Global Forum for Migration and Development (GFMD) is a voluntary and non-binding forum. It developed following a proposal associated with former UN Secretary-General Kofi Annan.\nHence, 2 and 3 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Colombo Process → Asian labour-sending countries → voluntary/non-binding.\nAbu Dhabi Dialogue → labour origin + destination countries → contractual labour mobility.\nGFMD → migration and development → voluntary/non-binding.\nUPSC trap: \"Consensus\" does not automatically mean binding."},
{"no":70,"text":"Consider the following UN organisations/agencies:\n1. World Food Programme\n2. United Nations Children's Fund\n3. United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees\n4. International Labour Organisation\nHow many of the above has/have been awarded the Nobel Prize twice?","A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Relations","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Only the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) has received the Nobel Peace Prize twice:\nUNHCR → 1954 and 1981 → twice.\nWorld Food Programme → 2020 → once.\nUNICEF → 1965 → once.\nInternational Labour Organisation → 1969 → once.\nTherefore, only one organisation among the four has received the Nobel Prize twice.","revisionPoints":"UNHCR → Nobel Peace Prize: 1954 + 1981\nUNICEF → 1965\nILO → 1969\nWFP → 2020\nQuick recall: Among the four listed, only UNHCR = twice."},
{"no":71,"text":"Match List I with List II and select the answer using the code given below.\nList I – UN Peacekeeping Operation\nA. UNMILB. MINURCATC. MINUSTAHD. UNMISET\nList II – Period of Operation\n2007–2010\n2002–2005\n2003–2018\n2004–2017\nCode:","A":"A-3 B-4 C-1 D-2","B":"A-3 B-1 C-4 D-2","C":"A-2 B-1 C-4 D-3","D":"A-2 B-4 C-1 D-3","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Relations","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"UNMIL (United Nations Mission in Liberia) → 2003–2018 → 3.\nMINURCAT (United Nations Mission in the Central African Republic and Chad) → 2007–2010 → 1.\nMINUSTAH (United Nations Stabilization Mission in Haiti) → 2004–2017 → 4.\nUNMISET (United Nations Mission of Support in East Timor) → 2002–2005 → 2.\nTherefore, the correct matching is A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2.","revisionPoints":"UNMIL → Liberia → 2003–2018\nMINURCAT → Chad/CAR → 2007–2010\nMINUSTAH → Haiti → 2004–2017\nUNMISET → East Timor → 2002–2005\nUPSC can test peacekeeping missions through acronym + country + operational period."},
{"no":72,"text":"Match List I with List II and select the answer using the code given below.\nList I – BIMSTEC Centre / Establishment\nA. BIMSTEC Cultural Industries ObservatoryB. BIMSTEC Energy CentreC. BIMSTEC Centre for Weather and ClimateD. BIMSTEC Technology Transfer Facility\nList II – Location\nNOIDA\nBengaluru\nColombo\nThimphu\nCode:","A":"A-3 B-2 C-1 D-4","B":"A-3 B-1 C-2 D-4","C":"A-4 B-2 C-1 D-3","D":"A-4 B-1 C-2 D-3","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Relations","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The correct matching is:\nBIMSTEC Cultural Industries Observatory → Thimphu, Bhutan\nBIMSTEC Energy Centre → Bengaluru, India\nBIMSTEC Centre for Weather and Climate → NOIDA, India\nBIMSTEC Technology Transfer Facility → Colombo, Sri Lanka\nThus, the correct combination is A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3.","revisionPoints":"Cultural Industries Observatory → Thimphu\nEnergy Centre → Bengaluru\nWeather and Climate Centre → NOIDA\nTechnology Transfer Facility → Colombo\nBIMSTEC connects countries around the Bay of Bengal and promotes regional cooperation."},
{"no":73,"text":"Which one of the following pairs is not correctly matched?\n1. Indian Army Corps\n2. Headquarters","A":"3 Corps","B":"4 Corps","C":"14 Corps","D":"33 Corps","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"3 Corps → Dimapur — correctly matched.\n4 Corps → Tezpur — correctly matched.\n14 Corps → Leh — correctly matched.\n33 Corps is headquartered at Sukna, West Bengal, not Srinagar.\nTherefore, (d) is the incorrectly matched pair.","revisionPoints":"3 Corps → Dimapur\n4 Corps → Tezpur\n14 Corps → Leh\n33 Corps → Sukna\nArmy Corps headquarters are important for understanding India's strategic military geography."},
{"no":74,"text":"Which of the following statements with respect to the Revamped Rashtriya Gram Swaraj Abhiyan (RGSA) is/are correct?\n1. The period of its implementation is 1st April, 2021 to 31st March, 2026.\n2. The key objective of the Revamped RGSA is to develop the governance capabilities of the Panchayati Raj Institutions to deliver on the Sustainable Development Goals.\n3. The share of the Central funding for the Revamped RGSA is 100% for all States and Union Territories.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Polity & Governance","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The Revamped RGSA was approved for the period 2022–23 to 2025–26, rather than from 1 April 2021.\nStatement 2 is correct: The scheme seeks to strengthen the governance capabilities of Panchayati Raj Institutions (PRIs) so that they can effectively achieve Sustainable Development Goals.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Central funding is not 100% for all States and UTs. The funding pattern varies according to the category of State/UT.\nTherefore, only Statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"RGSA → Panchayati Raj Institutions\nFocus → capacity building + institutional strengthening\nLinked with achievement of SDGs at the grassroots level.\nFunding pattern differs between:\nStates\nNorth-Eastern/Himalayan States\nUnion Territories\nUPSC trap → Do not assume 100% Central funding for every State/UT scheme."},
{"no":75,"text":"Which of the following countries are members of the European Union?\n1. Belarus\n2. Poland\n3. Germany\n4. Switzerland\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1, 2 and 4","B":"1 and 4 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"2 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Relations","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Poland is a member of the European Union.\nGermany is a founding member of the European integration process and an EU member.\nBelarus is not an EU member.\nSwitzerland is not an EU member, although it has extensive agreements and cooperation with the EU.\nHence, 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"EU members in the question: Poland, Germany.\nNot EU members: Belarus, Switzerland.\nSwitzerland ≠ EU member.\nEU membership should not be confused with:\nEuropean geographical location\nSchengen membership\nEuropean Free Trade Association membership."},
{"no":76,"text":"Match List I with List II and select the answer using the code given below.\nList I – INTERPOL Notice\nA. Silver NoticeB. Blue NoticeC. Black NoticeD. Green Notice\nList II – Description\nTo seek information on unidentified bodies\nTo collect additional information about a person's identity, location, or activities in relation to a criminal investigation\nTo provide warning about a person's criminal activities where the person is considered to be a possible threat to public safety\nTo identify and trace criminal assets\nCode:","A":"A-3 B-1 C-2 D-4","B":"A-3 B-2 C-1 D-4","C":"A-4 B-2 C-1 D-3","D":"A-4 B-1 C-2 D-3","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Relations","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Silver Notice → 4: Helps identify and trace criminal assets.\nBlue Notice → 2: Seeks additional information about a person's identity, location or activities in connection with a criminal investigation.\nBlack Notice → 1: Seeks information about unidentified bodies.\nGreen Notice → 3: Provides warnings about a person's criminal activities where they may pose a threat to public safety.\nTherefore, the correct matching is A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3.","revisionPoints":"Silver → Criminal assets\nBlue → Additional information\nBlack → Unidentified bodies\nGreen → Warning about criminal activities\nINTERPOL notices facilitate international police cooperation and information sharing."},
{"no":77,"text":"Which of the following statements in relation to NIRANTAR (National Institute for Research and Application of Natural Resources to Transform, Adapt and Build Resilience), a platform of institutions under the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change, is/are correct?\n1. Ecosystem Survey and Analysis is a vertical under this platform, the lead institute of which is Botanical Survey of India, Kolkata.\n2. Research and Management of Ecosystem Services is a vertical under this platform, the lead institute of which is Central Zoo Authority, New Delhi.\n3. Capacity Development Support is a vertical under this platform, the lead institute of which is Indian Institute of Forest Management, Bhopal.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environment & Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Ecosystem Survey and Analysis is a vertical under NIRANTAR. Its lead institute is Botanical Survey of India (BSI), Kolkata.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: Research and Management of Ecosystem Services is a vertical under NIRANTAR. But the Central Zoo Authority (CZA) is not the lead institute for this vertical.\nStatement 3 is correct: Indian Institute of Forest Management, Bhopal is the lead institute associated with the Capacity Development Support vertical.\nHence, only Statement 1 and 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"NIRANTAR → MoEFCC platform Platform of institutions under the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change.\nPurpose → Natural resources &amp; ecosystems Focuses on research, knowledge and institutional support.\nEcosystem Survey → BSI, Kolkata Botanical Survey of India (BSI), Kolkata is the lead institute.\nCapacity Development → IIFM, Bhopal Indian Institute of Forest Management (IIFM), Bhopal is the lead institute. CZA is NOT the lead for Ecosystem Services."},
{"no":78,"text":"The Chancellor of the Federal Republic of Germany visited India in January, 2026. Which of the following is/are not correct in terms of outcomes of this visit?\n1. Signing of a Memorandum of Understanding between the All India Institute of Ayurveda and the University of Hamburg.\n2. Signing of a Memorandum of Understanding on Youth Hockey Development between Hockey India and the German Hockey Federation.\n3. Establishment of a bilateral dialogue mechanism on the Indo-Pacific.\n4. Opening of an Honorary Consul of Germany in Lucknow.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"2 and 3","B":"1 and 4","C":"3 and 4","D":"1 only","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Relations","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is not correct: The stated MoU between the All India Institute of Ayurveda and the University of Hamburg was not among the outcomes of the visit as stated.\nStatement 2 is correct: Cooperation relating to youth hockey development between Hockey India and the German Hockey Federation was an outcome.\nStatement 3 is correct: India and Germany agreed to strengthen cooperation through a bilateral dialogue mechanism on the Indo-Pacific.\nStatement 4 is not correct: The stated opening of an honorary German consulate in Lucknow was not an outcome of the visit.\nTherefore, 1 and 4 are not correct.","revisionPoints":"India–Germany relations cover:\nStrategic cooperation\nDefence\nTechnology\nGreen development\nEducation\nSports\nIndo-Pacific cooperation\nCurrent-affairs questions can test specific outcomes of bilateral visits, rather than general relations.\nCarefully distinguish an actual outcome from a plausible-sounding proposal."},
{"no":79,"text":"Which of the following statements about DHRUV64 is/are correct?\n1. It is the third chip fabricated under the DIR-V Programme with an overall aim to enable the creation of microprocessors for India.\n2. It is India's first homegrown 10 GHz, 64-bit dual core microprocessor.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: DHRUV64 is associated with the Digital India RISC-V (DIR-V) Programme, which aims to develop indigenous processor technology.\nIt is the third chip fabricated under the programme.\nStatement 2 is correct: DHRUV64 is described as India's first homegrown 10 GHz, 64-bit dual-core microprocessor.\nThe development represents India's effort to build indigenous capabilities in processor and semiconductor technology.\nTherefore, both Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"DHRUV64 → indigenous microprocessor\nDIR-V → Digital India RISC-V\nKey specifications:\n64-bit\nDual-core\n10 GHz\nStrategic significance:\nSemiconductor self-reliance\nIndigenous computing\nReduced technological dependence\nRISC-V ecosystem development"},
{"no":80,"text":"The Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) recently introduced a national standard to test and assess bomb disposal system. Which of the following statements with regard to this system is/are correct?\n1. The new standard is known as IS 19445 : 2025.\n2. It will improve interoperability of equipment across agencies.\n3. It was developed by TBRL, DRDO in collaboration with the 30th Central Scientific Research Institute, Russia.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1 only","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) introduced a national standard for testing and assessing Bomb Disposal Systems. The standard is IS 19445:2025.\nStatement 2 is correct: The standard will help improve interoperability between equipment used by different agencies. Interoperability means different equipment/systems can work together effectively.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: The statement incorrectly says that the standard was developed by TBRL, DRDO in collaboration with the 30th Central Scientific Research Institute, Russia.\nTherefore,  Statement 1 and 2  is correct.","revisionPoints":"BIS → Bureau of Indian Standards\nBIS is India's national standards body.\nIS 19445:2025 → Standard for Bomb Disposal Systems\nPurpose → Testing and assessment of bomb disposal systems\nHelps ensure standardisation and interoperability.\nInteroperability → Different agencies/equipment can work together effectively\nImportant for security forces and bomb disposal operations.\nPrelims trap: Don't associate IS 19445:2025 with the Russian institute mentioned in Statement 3."},
{"no":81,"text":"'X', born in the UK, was conferred the Nobel Prize in 2025. He was a professor in an American university when this prize was announced. Identify 'X'.","A":"Michel H. Devoret","B":"Richard Robson","C":"John Clarke","D":"Joel Mokyr","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"John Clarke was born in the United Kingdom and was a professor at the University of California, Berkeley.\nHe was awarded the 2025 Nobel Prize in Physics along with Michel H. Devoret and John M. Martinis for discoveries concerning macroscopic quantum mechanical tunnelling and energy quantisation in an electric circuit.\nTherefore, the person described is John Clarke.","revisionPoints":"John Clarke → Nobel Prize in Physics 2025\nInstitution → University of California, Berkeley\nNobel Prize topic → Quantum effects in electrical circuits\nMichel H. Devoret was also a co-recipient.\nUPSC current affairs may connect scientist + institution + Nobel category + discovery."},
{"no":82,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to the Grand Slam Tennis Tournaments is/are correct?\n1. The tournaments have a shared governance structure establishing the partnership among the four Grand Slam tournaments.\n2. They are open for entry to all internationally ranked tennis players above the age of 14.\n3. There is a limitation on the number of 'Wild Cards' a player may receive to compete in a Grand Slam Tournament.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The four Grand Slam tournaments work together through a shared governance/partnership arrangement.\nStatement 2 is correct: Grand Slam events have eligibility and ranking requirements, with players meeting the applicable age and ranking criteria able to enter through the prescribed qualification and entry mechanisms.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: The rules do not establish the stated universal limitation on the number of Wild Cards that an individual player may receive across Grand Slam tournaments.\nHence, 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"Four Grand Slam tournaments:\nAustralian Open\nFrench Open\nWimbledon\nUS Open\nGrand Slams are the most prestigious tournaments in professional tennis.\nEntry may occur through:\ndirect ranking\nqualifying\nwild cards\nQuick recall: Grand Slam = 4 major tournaments."},
{"no":83,"text":"Which one of the following pairs of semiconductor plants in India and their locations is not correctly matched?","A":"CG Power and Industrial Solutions Pvt. Ltd. in partnership with Renesas Electronics and STARS Microelectronics — Gujarat","B":"Tata Semiconductor Assembly and Test Pvt. Ltd. — Assam","C":"HCL–Foxconn Joint Venture India Chip Ltd. — Madhya Pradesh","D":"SicSem Pvt. Ltd. — Odisha","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"CG Power and Industrial Solutions with its partners is associated with the semiconductor facility in Gujarat.\nTata Semiconductor Assembly and Test is establishing its semiconductor facility in Assam.\nSicSem Pvt. Ltd. is associated with a semiconductor facility in Odisha.\nThe HCL–Foxconn joint venture semiconductor facility is located in Uttar Pradesh, not Madhya Pradesh.\nTherefore, (c) is the incorrectly matched pair.","revisionPoints":"Semiconductor ecosystem locations are important current-affairs facts.\nTata → Assam\nCG Power consortium → Gujarat\nHCL–Foxconn → Uttar Pradesh\nSicSem → Odisha\nUPSC frequently tests project–company–State combinations."},
{"no":84,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to India's indigenous new high resolution weather model, the 'Bharat Forecast System', is/are correct?\n1. Its objective is to generate forecasts at the Panchayats cluster level.\n2. It was developed by IIT Delhi.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The Bharat Forecast System aims to provide highly localised weather forecasts, including forecasts useful at the Panchayat-cluster level.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: The system was developed by the Indian Institute of Tropical Meteorology (IITM), Pune, under the Ministry of Earth Sciences, rather than IIT Delhi.\nTherefore, only Statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Bharat Forecast System → high-resolution weather forecasting\nDeveloped by → IITM, Pune\nMinistry → Ministry of Earth Sciences\nFocus → highly localised forecasts.\nSignificance:\nagriculture\ndisaster management\nlocal planning\nweather-sensitive economic activities"},
{"no":85,"text":"Consider the following statements with regard to the film Boong:\n1. The film has recently won the British Academy of Film and Television Arts (BAFTA) Award in the Children's and Family Film category.\n2. The film is directed by Lakshmipriya Devi.\n3. This is the first Indian film to win a BAFTA award in the Children's and Family Film category.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"3 only","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Art & Culture","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Boong is a film associated with Manipur and was directed by Lakshmipriya Devi.\nIt received the BAFTA Award in the Children's and Family Film category.\nIts recognition was significant as it became the first Indian film to win in this BAFTA category.\nTherefore, all three statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Boong → Manipur\nDirector → Lakshmipriya Devi\nAward → BAFTA Children's and Family Film\nSignificance → first Indian film to win in this category.\nUPSC current affairs can combine cinema + regional identity + international awards."},
{"no":86,"text":"Which of the following statements regarding the features of blockchain technology are correct?\n1. Records stored in the database may be made visible to relevant stakeholders without risk of alteration.\n2. Copies of the entire database are stored on multiple computers on a network, syncing within seconds.\n3. Consortium blockchain is a blend of public and private blockchains allowing selective data access.\n4. Mathematical algorithms make it impossible to change or delete any data once recorded and accepted.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 3","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"1, 2 and 4","D":"1 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science & Technology","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Blockchain records can be shared among authorised or relevant participants, while its cryptographic and consensus mechanisms make unauthorised alteration extremely difficult.\nStatement 2 is correct: In distributed blockchain systems, copies of the ledger are maintained across multiple network participants and synchronised through the consensus mechanism.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: A consortium blockchain is a permissioned blockchain governed by a group of organisations. It is not simply a blend of public and private blockchains.\nStatement 4 is correct: Once data is accepted and recorded on a properly functioning blockchain, cryptographic linking and consensus make retrospective alteration extremely difficult.\nTherefore, 1, 2 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Blockchain → distributed ledger technology.\nCore features:\ndecentralisation/distribution\ncryptographic security\nconsensus\ntransparency/auditability\nimmutability/tamper resistance\nTypes:\nPublic\nPrivate\nConsortium\nQuick recall: Blockchain → distributed + cryptographically linked + tamper-resistant records."},
{"no":87,"text":"An e-commerce revenue model where the seller has control over pricing but doesn't keep products in stock and instead transfers customer orders and shipment details to a third-party supplier, who then ships the goods directly to the customer, is called:","A":"Dropshipping Model","B":"Affiliate Revenue Model","C":"Transaction Fee Revenue Model","D":"Agency Revenue Model","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"In dropshipping, the seller markets and sells products without maintaining physical inventory.\nWhen a customer places an order, the seller passes the order and shipping details to a third-party supplier.\nThe supplier then packages and ships the product directly to the customer.\nThe seller earns the difference between the selling price and the supplier's price, subject to other costs.\nTherefore, the correct answer is Dropshipping Model.","revisionPoints":"Dropshipping → seller does not hold inventory.\nSupplier → stores and ships the product.\nSeller → markets, prices and receives customer orders.\nAffiliate model → earns commission for referring customers.\nQuick recall: No inventory + third-party direct shipping = Dropshipping."},
{"no":88,"text":"Which one of the following correctly represents the three key sub-indices of the Financial Inclusion Index (FI-Index) of the Reserve Bank of India (RBI)?","A":"Credit access, Insurance depth and Pension coverage","B":"Banking access, GDP contribution and Financial literacy","C":"Access, Usage and Quality","D":"Access, Affordability and Transparency","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The RBI's Financial Inclusion Index (FI-Index) is based on three broad parameters:\nAccess → availability and accessibility of financial services.\nUsage → extent to which financial services are actually used.\nQuality → quality and effectiveness of financial services, including financial literacy and consumer protection.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (c) Access, Usage and Quality.","revisionPoints":"RBI FI-Index → 3 parameters\nAccess\nUsage\nQuality\nFI-Index scale → 0 to 100.\nFinancial inclusion means ensuring accessible, affordable and useful financial services for all sections."},
{"no":89,"text":"Which one of the following best describes the key objective of India's 'Open Network for Digital Commerce' (ONDC) initiative?","A":"To allow government control over all digital commerce transactions","B":"To replace private e-commerce players","C":"To break the dominance of large e-commerce platforms by enabling interoperability across networks","D":"To mandate UPI-based payments for all online transactions","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"ONDC is designed as an open, interoperable network for digital commerce.\nIt seeks to prevent dependence on a small number of dominant e-commerce platforms by allowing different buyers' and sellers' applications to interact across a common network.\nONDC does not seek to replace all private e-commerce companies.\nIt also does not mandate UPI as the only payment mechanism.\nTherefore, (c) is correct.","revisionPoints":"ONDC → Open Network for Digital Commerce\nCore idea → interoperability.\nObjectives:\nincrease competition\nwiden market access\nempower small sellers\nreduce platform dependence\nONDC ≠ an e-commerce marketplace itself.\nQuick recall: ONDC → network of networks."},
{"no":90,"text":"Which one of the following statements about Unified Payments Interface (UPI) and Central Bank Digital Currency (Digital Rupee) is not correct?","A":"UPI is a real-time payment system but Digital Rupee is akin to sovereign paper currency.","B":"In case of UPI, settlement for end users happens instantly as the money gets immediately debited or credited but in case of Digital Rupee, there is no settlement as the wallet balance gets transferred to another wallet.","C":"UPI transactions are recorded by banks and reflected in bank statements but in case of Digital Rupee, no data is captured in bank statements as transactions are from one wallet to another.","D":"In both the cases (UPI and Digital Rupee), the liability lies with the users and their respective banks.","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"UPI is a payment system that facilitates transfers between bank accounts. The underlying money remains a commercial-bank deposit.\nDigital Rupee (e₹) is a central bank digital currency (CBDC) issued by the RBI and represents a direct liability of the central bank.\nTherefore, the liability structure is different:\nUPI → commercial bank deposits\nDigital Rupee → RBI/central bank liability\nHence, statement (d) is not correct.\nThis distinction is fundamental: UPI is a payment mechanism, whereas the Digital Rupee is a form of digital money.","revisionPoints":"UPI\nPayment infrastructure\nTransfers bank-account money\nCommercial-bank deposit is the underlying liability\nDigital Rupee (e₹)\nCBDC\nIssued by RBI\nDirect liability of the central bank\nUPI ≠ Digital Rupee\nQuick recall: UPI = payment rail; e₹ = digital sovereign money."},
{"no":91,"text":"Which of the following statements about Real-World Asset (RWA) Tokenization are correct?\n1. Tokenization is the process of turning real world assets into digital tokens using blockchain technology.\n2. Tokenization of real world assets offers 24×7 access, promoting financial inclusion.\n3. Tokenization of real world assets will allow access to high growth investment opportunities for individuals in India.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Real-World Asset (RWA) tokenization refers to representing ownership or rights over a real-world asset through digital tokens recorded on a blockchain or distributed ledger.\nExamples of assets that may be tokenized include:\nReal estate\nGovernment or corporate bonds\nCommodities\nArt and collectibles\nOther financial or physical assets\nTokenization can potentially allow assets to be divided into smaller digital units, making participation possible with smaller amounts of capital. Blockchain-based systems can also enable transactions to occur continuously rather than being restricted to conventional market hours.\nHowever, tokenization itself does not automatically guarantee financial inclusion or high returns. These are potential benefits that depend on regulation, market infrastructure, liquidity, accessibility and the underlying asset.\nTherefore, in the context of the question, all three statements are considered correct.","revisionPoints":"RWA → Real-World Assets\nTokenization → Converting asset rights/ownership into digital tokens\nBlockchain → Distributed ledger technology\nPotential benefits → fractional ownership, programmability, transparency and greater accessibility\nTokenization ≠ cryptocurrency\nTokenization ≠ guaranteed investment returns\nRevise Blockchain → Smart Contracts → Tokenization → Digital Assets\nUPSC can connect RWA tokenization with fintech, blockchain, financial inclusion and digital finance."},
{"no":92,"text":"A bond whose proceeds are used only to finance or refinance a combination of both environmental and social projects is called:","A":"Green Bond","B":"Social Bond","C":"Sustainability Bond","D":"Sovereign Bond","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"A Sustainability Bond is a bond whose proceeds are used exclusively to finance or refinance a combination of eligible green and social projects.\nThe distinction is important:\nGreen Bond → environmental/green projects\nSocial Bond → social projects\nSustainability Bond → combination of green + social projects\nSovereign Bond → debt security issued by a government; it describes the issuer rather than the environmental/social use of proceeds.\nTherefore, the correct answer is Sustainability Bond.","revisionPoints":"Green Bond → Environmental projects\nSocial Bond → Social projects\nSustainability Bond → Green + Social projects\nSovereign Bond → Issued by government\nGreen projects → renewable energy, clean transport, energy efficiency etc.\nSocial projects → healthcare, education, affordable housing etc.\nDon't confuse purpose of bond proceeds with identity of issuer.\nUPSC can connect this topic with ESG, sustainable finance and climate finance."},
{"no":93,"text":"Which of the following statements about M1xchange's role in Micro, Small &amp; Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) financing is/are correct?\n1. M1xchange provides collateral based loans to MSMEs.\n2. M1xchange facilitates discounting of invoices and Bills of Exchange for MSMEs.\n3. M1xchange functions as a credit rating agency for MSMEs.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"M1xchange operates a Trade Receivables Discounting System (TReDS) platform. TReDS facilitates financing of MSME trade receivables through participating financiers.\nAn MSME can upload an eligible invoice. Once the buyer accepts the invoice, financiers can bid to discount the receivable. The MSME receives funds before the original payment due date, while the financier receives the payment later from the buyer. m1xchange.com\nTherefore:\nStatement 1 is incorrect — M1xchange's TReDS mechanism is based on invoice/receivable financing, not conventional collateral-based loans.\nStatement 2 is correct — discounting of invoices and Bills of Exchange is a core function of TReDS. m1xchange.com\nStatement 3 is incorrect — M1xchange is not a credit rating agency.","revisionPoints":"TReDS → Trade Receivables Discounting System\nPurpose → MSMEs obtain finance against trade receivables\nParticipants → MSME sellers + buyers + financiers\nFinanciers → Banks, NBFC-Factors and permitted financial institutions\nInvoice discounting → Immediate liquidity against receivables\nTReDS → RBI-regulated framework\nM1xchange → TReDS platform\nCredit rating agency ≠ TReDS platform\nUPSC can ask about factoring, invoice discounting, reverse factoring and TReDS."},
{"no":94,"text":"Which one of the following best describes the \"Crowding Out Effect\" in the context of fiscal policy?","A":"A situation where private investment increases due to increased Government spending.","B":"A situation where Government borrowing leads to higher interest rates, which reduces private investment.","C":"A situation where an increase in taxes leads to increased private sector investment.","D":"A situation where Government spending has no impact on aggregate demand.","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"The crowding out effect occurs when increased government borrowing puts upward pressure on interest rates and consequently reduces private-sector borrowing and investment.\nFor example:\nHigher Government borrowing → Greater demand for funds → Possible rise in interest rates → Cost of private borrowing increases → Private investment may decline\nThus, government borrowing can crowd out private investment.\nThe extent of crowding out depends on the economic environment. It may be weaker during periods of substantial spare capacity or accommodative monetary conditions.","revisionPoints":"Crowding Out → Government borrowing can reduce private investment\nKey mechanism → Higher demand for loanable funds → Higher interest rates\nOpposite concept → Crowding In\nFiscal policy → Government taxation and expenditure\nMonetary policy → Money supply/interest-rate conditions\nCrowding out is particularly associated with expansionary fiscal policy financed through borrowing.\nUPSC can connect it with fiscal deficit, public debt, interest rates and private investment."},
{"no":95,"text":"Which of the following statements about Rare Earth Elements (REEs) and Critical Minerals is/are correct?\n1. Modern technological innovations including Artificial Intelligence, robotics and space exploration extensively utilise Rare Earth Elements (REEs).\n2. China has the highest share in mining of REEs followed by India.\n3. The Government of India launched the National Critical Mineral Mission (NCMM) in 2025 to establish a robust framework for self-reliance in the critical mineral sector.\n4. Rare Earth Elements are a set of 13 metallic elements.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"3 only","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct.\nRare Earth Elements are important in several advanced technologies, including electronics, permanent magnets, electric motors, defence systems, renewable-energy technologies and advanced manufacturing. Their importance extends to technologies associated with AI infrastructure, robotics and space applications.\nStatement 2 is incorrect.\nChina is a major global producer of rare earths, but the statement that India follows China as the second-largest mining producer is incorrect. India's importance is significant in terms of resources and strategic potential, but it cannot be placed second simply on the basis stated.\nStatement 3 is correct.\nThe Government of India launched the National Critical Mineral Mission (NCMM) in 2025 to strengthen India's critical-mineral ecosystem and reduce import dependence. The government has also assigned major exploration responsibilities to agencies including the Geological Survey of India. Press Information Bureau\nStatement 4 is incorrect.\nThere are 17 Rare Earth Elements, not 13:\n15 lanthanides\nScandium\nYttrium\nTherefore, the correct answer is (a).","revisionPoints":"Rare Earth Elements → 17 elements\n15 lanthanides + Scandium + Yttrium\nImportant uses → magnets, electronics, defence, renewable energy, advanced technologies\nChina → dominant global player in rare-earth supply chains\nCritical minerals ≠ only rare earth elements\nCritical minerals include several other minerals such as lithium, cobalt, nickel and graphite.\nNCMM → launched in 2025\nKey objective → reduce import dependence and strengthen domestic capabilities\nUPSC can connect this with critical minerals + energy transition + EVs + semiconductor/technology supply chains + national security."},
{"no":96,"text":"Which of the following statements about insurance in aviation sector is/are correct?\n1. \"Aviation Hull Insurance\" covers the physical aircraft, including the body, engine and on-board equipment.\n2. Under the Montreal Convention, adopted in 1999 by over 130 countries, including India, airlines are strictly liable to pay compensation to the deceased family/nominee of every deceased passenger without requiring the family to prove fault.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct.\nAviation Hull Insurance protects against physical loss or damage to the aircraft itself. It generally covers the aircraft structure and may include components such as the engine and equipment, subject to the policy terms.\nIt is different from aviation liability insurance, which deals with liabilities arising from injury, death or property damage involving third parties/passengers.\nStatement 2 is based on the strict-liability framework of the Montreal Convention.\nThe Montreal Convention, 1999 establishes a framework for international carriage by air and provides a system of carrier liability for passenger injury or death.\nAn important UPSC nuance is that the liability system has different tiers. The carrier is strictly liable up to the applicable first-tier threshold, while liability beyond that threshold is subject to conditions and defences provided under the Convention.\nTherefore, the statement is treated as correct in the context of the question.","revisionPoints":"Aviation Hull Insurance → Physical aircraft\nAviation Liability Insurance → Liability toward passengers/third parties\nMontreal Convention → International carriage by air\nAdopted → 1999\nIt modernised the international framework governing airline liability.\nRevise distinction between strict liability and fault-based liability.\nUPSC can connect this with international conventions, civil aviation and insurance."},
{"no":97,"text":"Which of the following statements about crowdfunding is/are correct?\n1. Crowdfunding is solicitation of funds from small amounts from multiple investors through a web-based platform or social networking site for a specific project.\n2. Small and Medium Enterprises (SMEs) are able to raise funds at lower cost of capital without undergoing rigorous procedures.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Crowdfunding involves raising funds from a large number of individuals, generally with each contributor providing a relatively small amount.\nDigital platforms make it possible to connect:\nProject/Business → Online Platform → Large Number of Contributors\nCrowdfunding can provide an alternative source of finance for start-ups, entrepreneurs and SMEs, particularly when access to conventional financing is difficult.\nHowever, crowdfunding is not completely free from regulation or risk. The applicable regulatory framework depends on the type of crowdfunding and jurisdiction.","revisionPoints":"Crowdfunding → Many contributors + relatively small contributions\nMain medium → Online platforms\nUses → Business, projects, creative ventures, social causes\nPotential benefit → Alternative source of finance\nMajor forms → Donation-based, reward-based, debt-based, equity-based\nEquity crowdfunding → investors receive ownership/equity\nReward crowdfunding → contributor receives a product/reward\nDon't confuse crowdfunding with crowdfunding platforms used for donations.\nUPSC can connect crowdfunding with fintech, financial inclusion, start-ups and capital markets."},
{"no":98,"text":"Consider the following Committees in India and the following details:\n1. Sl. No.\n2. Committee\n3. Objective\n4. Organization\n5. 1\n6. R.N. Malhotra Committee\n7. Comprehensive reforms of Insurance sector\n8. IRDAI\n9. 2\n10. L.C. Gupta Committee\n11. Roadmap for introduction of derivatives trading\n12. SEBI\n13. 3\n14. Urjit R. Patel Committee\n15. Roadmap for reforming bank lending to housing sector\n16. RBI\n17. 4\n18. Y.H. Malegam Committee\n19. Roadmap for reforms in Microfinance sector\n20. RBI\nIn which of the above rows are all the details correctly matched?","A":"2 only","B":"2 and 3","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"2 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Row 1 is incorrect.\nThe R.N. Malhotra Committee was constituted by the Government of India in 1993 to recommend reforms in the insurance sector. IRDAI was subsequently established as part of the insurance-sector reform process. Therefore, attributing formation of the committee to IRDAI is incorrect.\nRow 2 is correct.\nThe L.C. Gupta Committee was associated with the development and regulation of derivatives markets in India and was constituted by SEBI. SEBI documents continue to refer to the committee's recommendations on derivatives. Securities and Exchange Board of India\nRow 3 is incorrect.\nThe Urjit R. Patel Committee was associated primarily with the monetary policy framework, rather than the stated roadmap for reforming bank lending to the housing sector.\nRow 4 is correct.\nThe Y.H. Malegam Committee was constituted by the RBI to examine issues and concerns in the microfinance sector. Press Information Bureau\nTherefore, only Rows 2 and 4 are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"R.N. Malhotra Committee → Insurance-sector reforms\nL.C. Gupta Committee → Derivatives\nUrjit R. Patel Committee → Monetary policy framework\nY.H. Malegam Committee → Microfinance\nSEBI → Securities market\nRBI → Monetary policy and banking regulation\nIRDAI → Insurance-sector regulator\nUPSC frequently tests Committee → Subject → Parent institution combinations.\nPay special attention when a question says \"constituted by\" versus \"recommendations implemented by\"."},
{"no":99,"text":"Consider the following statements about Non-Banking Financial Companies (NBFCs) in India:\n1. NBFCs cannot accept demand deposits.\n2. All the NBFCs operating in India have to be registered with the RBI.\n3. NBFCs form part of the payment and settlement system and can issue cheque drawn on itself.\n4. Deposit insurance facility of Deposit Insurance and Credit Guarantee Corporation (DICGC) is not available to the depositors of deposit-taking NBFCs.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 4","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"4 only","D":"2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct.\nNBFCs cannot accept demand deposits like banks. Their deposit-taking capacity is subject to specific regulatory conditions.\nStatement 2 is incorrect as worded.\nThe word \"all\" is important. While NBFCs generally require registration with the RBI under the applicable framework, there are specific categories/entities and statutory exemptions. UPSC often uses absolute words such as all, always, only and never as traps.\nStatement 3 is incorrect.\nNBFCs do not form part of the payment and settlement system in the same manner as banks, and an NBFC cannot issue a cheque drawn on itself in the way a bank can.\nStatement 4 is correct.\nThe DICGC deposit-insurance facility is not available to deposits of NBFCs. DICGC insurance is primarily associated with eligible bank deposits.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (a).","revisionPoints":"NBFC → Non-Banking Financial Company\nNBFCs perform several financial activities but are not banks.\nNBFC ≠ Bank\nNBFCs cannot accept demand deposits.\nDICGC → Deposit insurance\nDICGC insurance → Eligible bank deposits, subject to applicable rules\nBanks → Part of payment and settlement system\nUPSC trap words → All, only, always, never\nRevise differences between Banks vs NBFCs."},
{"no":100,"text":"Consider the following statements about Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI):\n1. MPI is calculated using Alkire-Foster methodology.\n2. MPI calculated by NITI Aayog has a total of twelve indicators.\n3. Maternal Health and Bank Account are common indicators in the MPI of NITI Aayog and MPI of United Nations Development Programme (UNDP).\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"2 only","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Economy","tags":["UPSC","2026"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct.\nThe Alkire-Foster methodology is used to identify and measure multidimensional poverty by considering multiple deprivations rather than income alone. NITI Aayog's National MPI uses this methodology. NITI Aayog\nStatement 2 is correct.\nNITI Aayog's National MPI uses 12 indicators under three dimensions:\nHealth\nNutrition\nChild &amp; Adolescent Mortality\nMaternal Health\nEducation\nYears of Schooling\nSchool Attendance\nStandard of Living\nCooking Fuel\nSanitation\nDrinking Water\nHousing\nElectricity\nAssets\nBank Account\nNITI Aayog explicitly states that India's National MPI retains the 10 indicators of the global MPI and adds Maternal Health and Bank Account, giving a total of 12 indicators. NITI Aayog\nStatement 3 is incorrect.\nMaternal Health and Bank Account were additional indicators introduced in India's National MPI. They are not common indicators shared by the global UNDP MPI in the same way.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (a) 1 and 2 only.","revisionPoints":"MPI → Multidimensional Poverty Index\nAlkire-Foster → methodology\nNITI Aayog National MPI → 12 indicators\nThree dimensions:\nHealth\nEducation\nStandard of Living\nNITI Aayog added:\nMaternal Health\nBank Account\nGlobal MPI → 10 indicators\nNational MPI → 12 indicators\nMPI looks beyond income and measures multiple deprivations.\nImportant distinction:\nHeadcount Ratio → How many are poor?\nIntensity → How poor are the poor?\nUPSC can ask about MPI indicators, dimensions, methodology and differences between India's National MPI and Global MPI."},
];
